window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2019-06-1", "source": "2019年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of team spirit and communication in the workplace. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "In today's increasingly collaborative workplaces, no employee works in isolation. To my mind, team spirit and\neffective communication are two cornerstones of professional success, and neither can be neglected.\nFor one thing, team spirit binds individuals into a united force. When colleagues share a common goal and trust\none another, they are willing to pool their strengths and cover each other's weaknesses. A software project, for\ninstance, can rarely be completed by a lone programmer; only when designers, coders and testers coordinate\ntheir efforts can a difficult task be conquered on time.\nFor another, effective communication keeps this force running smoothly. Clear and timely exchange of\ninformation prevents misunderstandings, resolves conflicts before they escalate, and ensures that everyone\nstays on the same page. Even the most talented team will fall apart if its members merely guess at each other's\nintentions instead of speaking openly.\nIn conclusion, team spirit and communication reinforce each other and together form the bedrock of workplace\nachievement. Any organization hoping to thrive should therefore nurture a cooperative atmosphere and\nencourage open dialogue among its staff."}, "translation": {"prompt": "成语(Chinese idioms)是汉语中的一种独特的表达方式，大多由四个汉字组成。它们高度简练且形式固定，但通常能形象地表达深刻的含义。成语大多数来源于中国古代的文学作品，通常与某些神话、传说或者历史事件有关。如果不知道某个成语的出处，就很难理解其确切含义。因为，学习成语有助于人们更好地理解中国传统文化。成语在日常会话和文学创作中广泛使用。恰当使用成语可以使一个人的语言更具表现力，交流更有效。", "sample": "Chinese idioms are a unique form of expression in Chinese, and most of them are made up of four characters. They are highly concise and fixed in form, but they can usually express profound meanings vividly. Most idioms originate from ancient Chinese literary works, and they are often related to certain myths, legends or historical events. If you do not know the origin of an idiom, it is difficult to understand its exact meaning. Therefore, learning idioms helps people better understand traditional Chinese culture. Idioms are widely used in daily conversation and literary creation. The proper use of idioms can make one's language more expressive and communication more effective."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2019-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Pasta is no longer off the menu, after a new review of studies suggested that the carbohydrate can form part of a healthy diet, and even help people lose weight. For years, nutritionists have recommended that pasta be kept to a  __26__  , to cut calories, prevent fat build-up and stop blood sugar  __27__  up.\nThe low-carbohydrate food movement gave birth to such diets as the Atkins, Paleo and Keto, which advised swapping foods like bread, pasta and potatoes for vegetables, fish and meat. More recently the trend of swapping spaghetti for vegetables has been  __28__  by clean-eating experts.\nBut now a  __29__  review and analysis of 30 studies by Canadian researchers found that not only does pasta not cause weight gain, but three meals a week can help people drop more than half a kilogram over four months. The reviewers found that pasta had been unfairly demonized(妖魔化) because it had been  __30__  in with other, more fat-promoting carbohydrates.\n\"The study found that pasta didn't  __31__  to weight gain or increase in body fat,\" said lead author Dr. John Sievenpiper. \"In  __32__  the evidence, we can now say with some confidence that pasta does not have an  __33__  effect on body weight outcomes when it is consumed as part of a healthy dietary pattern.\" In fact, analysis actually showed a small weight loss  __34__  to concerns. Perhaps pasta can be part of a healthy diet.\nThose involved in the  __35__  trials on average ate 3.3 servings of pasta a week instead of other carbohydrates, one serving equaling around half a cup. They lost around half a kilogram over an average follow-up of 12 weeks.", "matchIntro": "The Best Retailers Combine Bricks and Clicks", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Retail profits are falling sharply. Stores are closing. Malls are emptying. The depressing stories just keep coming. Reading the earnings announcements of large retail stores like Macy's, Nordstrom, and Target is about as uplifting as a tour of an intensive care unit. The internet is apparently taking down yet another industry. Brick and mortar stores(实体店) seem to be going the way of the yellow pages. Sure enough, the Census Bureau just released data showing that online retail sales surged 15.2 percent between the first quarter of 2015 and the first quarter of 2016."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But before you dump all of your retail stocks, there are more facts you should consider. Looking only at that 15.2 percent \"surge\" would be misleading. It was an increase that was on a small base of 6.9 percent. Even when a tiny number grows by a large percentage terms, it is often still tiny."}, {"label": "C", "text": "More than 20 years after the internet was opened to commerce, the Census Bureau tells us that brick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales in the first quarter of 2016. Their data show that only 0.8 percent of retail sales shifted from offline to online between the beginning of 2015 and 2016."}, {"label": "D", "text": "So, despite all the talk about drone(无人机) deliveries to your doorstep, all the retail executives expressing anxiety over consumers going online, and even a Presidential candidate exclaiming that Amazon has a \"huge antitrust problem,\" the Census data suggest that physical retail is thriving. Of course, the closed stores, depressed executives, and sinking stocks suggest otherwise. What's the real story?"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Many firms operating brick and mortar stores are in trouble. The retail industry is getting reinvented, as we describe in our new book Matchmakers. It's standing in the path of what Schumpeter called a gale(大风) of creative destruction. That storm has been brewing for some time, and as it has reached gale force, most large retailers are searching for a response. As the CFO of Macy's put it recently, \"We're frankly scratching our heads.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "But it's not happening as experts predicted. In the peak of the dot-com bubble, brick and mortar retail was one of those industries the internet was going to kill—and quickly. The dot-com bust discredited most predictions of that sort and in the years that followed, conventional retailers' confidence in the future increased as Census continued to report weak online sales. And then the gale hit."}, {"label": "G", "text": "It is becoming increasingly clear that retail reinvention isn't a simple battle to the death between bricks and clicks. It is about devising retail models that work for people who are making increasing use of a growing array of internet-connected tools to change how they search, shop, and buy. Creative retailers are using the new technologies to innovate just about everything stores do from managing inventory, to marketing, to getting paid."}, {"label": "H", "text": "More than drones dropping a new supply of underwear on your doorstep, Apple's massively successful brick-and-mortar-and-glass retail stores and Amazon's small steps in the same direction are what should keep old-fashioned retailers awake at night. Not to mention the large number of creative new retailers, like Bonobos, that are blending online and offline experiences in creative ways."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Retail reinvention is not a simple process, and it's also not happening on what used to be called \"Internet Time.\" Some internet-driven changes have happened quickly, of course. Craigslist quickly overtook newspaper classified ads and turned newspaper economics upside down. But many widely anticipated changes weren't quick, and some haven't really started. With the benefit of hindsight(后见之明), it looks like the internet will transform the economy at something like the pace of other great inventions like electricity. B2B commerce, for example, didn't move mainly online by 2005 as many had predicted in 2000, nor even by 2016, but that doesn't mean it won't do so over the next few decades."}, {"label": "J", "text": "But the gale is still blowing. The sudden decline in foot traffic in recent years, even though it hasn't been accompanied by a massive decline in physical sales, is a critical warning. People can shop more efficiently online and therefore don't need to go to as many stores to find what they want. There's a surplus of physical shopping space for the crowds, which is one reason why stores are downsizing and closing."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The rise of the mobile phone has recently added a new level of complexity to the process of retail reinvention. Even five years ago most people faced a choice. Sit at your computer, probably at home or at the office, search and browse, and buy. Or head out to the mall, or Main Street, look and shop, and buy. Now, just about everyone has a smartphone, connected to the internet almost everywhere almost all the time. Even when a retailer gets a customer to walk in the store, she can easily see if there's a better deal online or at another store nearby."}, {"label": "L", "text": "So far, the main thing many large retailers have done in response to all this is to open online stores, so people will come to them directly rather than to Amazon and its smaller online rivals. Many are having the same problem that newspapers have. Even if they get online traffic, they struggle to make enough money online to compensate for what they are losing offline."}, {"label": "M", "text": "A few seem to be making this work. Among large traditional retailers, Walmart recently reported the best results, leading its stock price to surge, while Macy's, Target, and Nordstrom's dropped. Yet Walmart's year-over-year online sales only grew 7 percent, leading its CEO to lament(哀叹), \"Growth here is too slow.\" Part of the problem is that almost two decades after Amazon filed the one-click patent, the online retail shopping and buying experience is filled with frictions. A recent study graded more than 600 internet retailers on how easy it was for consumers to shop, buy, and pay. Almost half of the sites didn't get a passing grade and only 18 percent got an A or B."}, {"label": "N", "text": "The turmoil on the ground in physical retail is hard to square with the Census data. Unfortunately, part of the explanation is that the Census retail data are unreliable. Our deep look into those data and their preparation revealed serious problems. It seems likely that Census simply misclassifies a large chunk of online sales. It is certain that the Census procedures, which lump the online sales of major traditional retailers like Walmart with \"non-store retailers\" like food trucks can mask major changes in individual retail categories. The bureau could easily present their data in more useful ways. But they have chosen not to."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Despite the turmoil, brick and mortar won't disappear any time soon. The big questions are which, if any, of the large traditional retailers will still be on the scene in a decade or two because they have successfully reinvented themselves, which new players will operate busy stores on Main Streets and maybe even in shopping malls, and how the shopping and buying experience will have changed in each retail category. Investors shouldn't write off brick and mortar. Whether they should bet on the traditional players who run those stores now is another matter."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Professor Stephen Hawking has warned that the creation of powerful artificial intelligence (AI) will be \"either the best, or the worst thing, ever to happen to humanity\", and praised the creation of an academic institute dedicated to researching the future of intelligence as \"crucial to the future of our civilisation and our species\".\nHawking was speaking at the opening of the Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence (LCFI) at Cambridge University, a multi-disciplinary institute that will attempt to tackle some of the open-ended questions raised by the rapid pace of development in AI research. \"We spend a great deal of time studying in history,\" Hawking said, \"which, let's face it, is mostly the history of stupidity. So it's a welcome change that people are studying instead the future of intelligence.\"\nWhile the world-renowned physicist has often been cautious about AI, raising concerns that humanity could be the architect of its own destruction if it creates a super-intelligence with a will of its own, he was also quick to highlight the positives that AI research can bring. \"The potential benefits of creating intelligence are huge,\" he said. \"We cannot predict what we might achieve when our own minds are amplified by AI. Perhaps with the tools of this new technological revolution, we will be able to undo some of the damage done to the natural world by the last one—industrialisation. And surely we will aim to finally eradicate disease and poverty. And every aspect of our lives will be transformed. In short, success in creating AI could be the biggest event in the history of our civilisation.\"\nHuw Price, the centre's academic director and the Bertrand Russell professor of philosophy at Cambridge University, where Hawking is also an academic, said that the centre came about partially as a result of the university's Centre for Existential Risk. That institute examined a wider range of potential problems for humanity, while the LCFI has a narrow focus.\nAI pioneer Margaret Boden, professor of cognitive science at the University of Sussex, praised the progress of such discussions. As recently as 2009, she said, the topic wasn't taken seriously, even among AI researchers. \"AI is hugely exciting,\" she said, \"but it has limitations, which present grave dangers given uncritical use.\"\nThe academic community is not alone in warning about the potential dangers of AI as well as the potential benefits. A number of pioneers from the technology industry, most famously the entrepreneur Elon Musk, have also expressed their concerns about the damage that a super-intelligent AI could do to humanity.", "47": "Professor Stephen Hawking has warned that the creation of powerful artificial intelligence (AI) will be \"either the best, or the worst thing, ever to happen to humanity\", and praised the creation of an academic institute dedicated to researching the future of intelligence as \"crucial to the future of our civilisation and our species\".\nHawking was speaking at the opening of the Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence (LCFI) at Cambridge University, a multi-disciplinary institute that will attempt to tackle some of the open-ended questions raised by the rapid pace of development in AI research. \"We spend a great deal of time studying in history,\" Hawking said, \"which, let's face it, is mostly the history of stupidity. So it's a welcome change that people are studying instead the future of intelligence.\"\nWhile the world-renowned physicist has often been cautious about AI, raising concerns that humanity could be the architect of its own destruction if it creates a super-intelligence with a will of its own, he was also quick to highlight the positives that AI research can bring. \"The potential benefits of creating intelligence are huge,\" he said. \"We cannot predict what we might achieve when our own minds are amplified by AI. Perhaps with the tools of this new technological revolution, we will be able to undo some of the damage done to the natural world by the last one—industrialisation. And surely we will aim to finally eradicate disease and poverty. And every aspect of our lives will be transformed. In short, success in creating AI could be the biggest event in the history of our civilisation.\"\nHuw Price, the centre's academic director and the Bertrand Russell professor of philosophy at Cambridge University, where Hawking is also an academic, said that the centre came about partially as a result of the university's Centre for Existential Risk. That institute examined a wider range of potential problems for humanity, while the LCFI has a narrow focus.\nAI pioneer Margaret Boden, professor of cognitive science at the University of Sussex, praised the progress of such discussions. As recently as 2009, she said, the topic wasn't taken seriously, even among AI researchers. \"AI is hugely exciting,\" she said, \"but it has limitations, which present grave dangers given uncritical use.\"\nThe academic community is not alone in warning about the potential dangers of AI as well as the potential benefits. A number of pioneers from the technology industry, most famously the entrepreneur Elon Musk, have also expressed their concerns about the damage that a super-intelligent AI could do to humanity.", "48": "Professor Stephen Hawking has warned that the creation of powerful artificial intelligence (AI) will be \"either the best, or the worst thing, ever to happen to humanity\", and praised the creation of an academic institute dedicated to researching the future of intelligence as \"crucial to the future of our civilisation and our species\".\nHawking was speaking at the opening of the Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence (LCFI) at Cambridge University, a multi-disciplinary institute that will attempt to tackle some of the open-ended questions raised by the rapid pace of development in AI research. \"We spend a great deal of time studying in history,\" Hawking said, \"which, let's face it, is mostly the history of stupidity. So it's a welcome change that people are studying instead the future of intelligence.\"\nWhile the world-renowned physicist has often been cautious about AI, raising concerns that humanity could be the architect of its own destruction if it creates a super-intelligence with a will of its own, he was also quick to highlight the positives that AI research can bring. \"The potential benefits of creating intelligence are huge,\" he said. \"We cannot predict what we might achieve when our own minds are amplified by AI. Perhaps with the tools of this new technological revolution, we will be able to undo some of the damage done to the natural world by the last one—industrialisation. And surely we will aim to finally eradicate disease and poverty. And every aspect of our lives will be transformed. In short, success in creating AI could be the biggest event in the history of our civilisation.\"\nHuw Price, the centre's academic director and the Bertrand Russell professor of philosophy at Cambridge University, where Hawking is also an academic, said that the centre came about partially as a result of the university's Centre for Existential Risk. That institute examined a wider range of potential problems for humanity, while the LCFI has a narrow focus.\nAI pioneer Margaret Boden, professor of cognitive science at the University of Sussex, praised the progress of such discussions. As recently as 2009, she said, the topic wasn't taken seriously, even among AI researchers. \"AI is hugely exciting,\" she said, \"but it has limitations, which present grave dangers given uncritical use.\"\nThe academic community is not alone in warning about the potential dangers of AI as well as the potential benefits. A number of pioneers from the technology industry, most famously the entrepreneur Elon Musk, have also expressed their concerns about the damage that a super-intelligent AI could do to humanity.", "49": "Professor Stephen Hawking has warned that the creation of powerful artificial intelligence (AI) will be \"either the best, or the worst thing, ever to happen to humanity\", and praised the creation of an academic institute dedicated to researching the future of intelligence as \"crucial to the future of our civilisation and our species\".\nHawking was speaking at the opening of the Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence (LCFI) at Cambridge University, a multi-disciplinary institute that will attempt to tackle some of the open-ended questions raised by the rapid pace of development in AI research. \"We spend a great deal of time studying in history,\" Hawking said, \"which, let's face it, is mostly the history of stupidity. So it's a welcome change that people are studying instead the future of intelligence.\"\nWhile the world-renowned physicist has often been cautious about AI, raising concerns that humanity could be the architect of its own destruction if it creates a super-intelligence with a will of its own, he was also quick to highlight the positives that AI research can bring. \"The potential benefits of creating intelligence are huge,\" he said. \"We cannot predict what we might achieve when our own minds are amplified by AI. Perhaps with the tools of this new technological revolution, we will be able to undo some of the damage done to the natural world by the last one—industrialisation. And surely we will aim to finally eradicate disease and poverty. And every aspect of our lives will be transformed. In short, success in creating AI could be the biggest event in the history of our civilisation.\"\nHuw Price, the centre's academic director and the Bertrand Russell professor of philosophy at Cambridge University, where Hawking is also an academic, said that the centre came about partially as a result of the university's Centre for Existential Risk. That institute examined a wider range of potential problems for humanity, while the LCFI has a narrow focus.\nAI pioneer Margaret Boden, professor of cognitive science at the University of Sussex, praised the progress of such discussions. As recently as 2009, she said, the topic wasn't taken seriously, even among AI researchers. \"AI is hugely exciting,\" she said, \"but it has limitations, which present grave dangers given uncritical use.\"\nThe academic community is not alone in warning about the potential dangers of AI as well as the potential benefits. A number of pioneers from the technology industry, most famously the entrepreneur Elon Musk, have also expressed their concerns about the damage that a super-intelligent AI could do to humanity.", "50": "Professor Stephen Hawking has warned that the creation of powerful artificial intelligence (AI) will be \"either the best, or the worst thing, ever to happen to humanity\", and praised the creation of an academic institute dedicated to researching the future of intelligence as \"crucial to the future of our civilisation and our species\".\nHawking was speaking at the opening of the Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence (LCFI) at Cambridge University, a multi-disciplinary institute that will attempt to tackle some of the open-ended questions raised by the rapid pace of development in AI research. \"We spend a great deal of time studying in history,\" Hawking said, \"which, let's face it, is mostly the history of stupidity. So it's a welcome change that people are studying instead the future of intelligence.\"\nWhile the world-renowned physicist has often been cautious about AI, raising concerns that humanity could be the architect of its own destruction if it creates a super-intelligence with a will of its own, he was also quick to highlight the positives that AI research can bring. \"The potential benefits of creating intelligence are huge,\" he said. \"We cannot predict what we might achieve when our own minds are amplified by AI. Perhaps with the tools of this new technological revolution, we will be able to undo some of the damage done to the natural world by the last one—industrialisation. And surely we will aim to finally eradicate disease and poverty. And every aspect of our lives will be transformed. In short, success in creating AI could be the biggest event in the history of our civilisation.\"\nHuw Price, the centre's academic director and the Bertrand Russell professor of philosophy at Cambridge University, where Hawking is also an academic, said that the centre came about partially as a result of the university's Centre for Existential Risk. That institute examined a wider range of potential problems for humanity, while the LCFI has a narrow focus.\nAI pioneer Margaret Boden, professor of cognitive science at the University of Sussex, praised the progress of such discussions. As recently as 2009, she said, the topic wasn't taken seriously, even among AI researchers. \"AI is hugely exciting,\" she said, \"but it has limitations, which present grave dangers given uncritical use.\"\nThe academic community is not alone in warning about the potential dangers of AI as well as the potential benefits. A number of pioneers from the technology industry, most famously the entrepreneur Elon Musk, have also expressed their concerns about the damage that a super-intelligent AI could do to humanity.", "51": "The market for products designed specifically for older adults could reach $30 billion by next year, and startups(初创公司) want in on the action. What they sometimes lack is feedback from the people who they hope will use their products. So Brookdale, the country's largest owner of retirement communities, has been inviting a few select entrepreneurs just to move in for a few days, show off their products and hear what the residents have to say.\nThat's what brought Dayle Rodriguez, 28, all the way from England to the dining room of Brookdale South Bay in Torrance, California. Rodriguez is the community and marketing manager for a company called Sentab. The startup's product, SentabTV, enables older adults who may not be comfortable with computers to access email, video chat and social media using just their televisions and a remote control.\n\"It's nothing new, it's nothing too complicated and it's natural because lots of people have TV remotes,\" says Rodriguez.\nBut none of that is the topic of conversation in the Brookdale dining room. Instead, Rodriguez solicits residents' advice on what he should get on his cheeseburger and how he should spend the afternoon. Playing cards was on the agenda, as well as learning to play mahjong(麻将).\nRodriguez says it's important that residents here don't feel like he's selling them something. \"I've had more feedback in a passive approach,\" he says. \"Playing pool, playing cards, having dinner, having lunch,\" all work better \"than going through a survey of questions. When they get to know me and to trust me, knowing for sure I'm not selling them something—there'll be more honest feedback from them.\"\nRodriguez is just the seventh entrepreneur to move into one of Brookdale's 1,100 senior living communities. Other new products in the program have included a kind of full-body blow dryer and specially designed clothing that allows people with disabilities to dress and undress themselves.\nMary Lou Busch, 93, agreed to try the Sentab system. She tells Rodriguez that it might be good for someone, but not for her. \"I have the computer and FaceTime, which I talk with my family on,\" she explains. She also has an iPad and a smartphone. \"So I do pretty much everything I need to do.\"\nTo be fair, if Rodriguez had wanted feedback from some more technophobic(害怕技术的) seniors, he might have ended up in the wrong Brookdale community. This one is located in the heart of Southern California's aerospace corridor. Many residents have backgrounds in engineering, business and academic circles.\nBut Rodriguez says he's still learning something important by moving into this Brookdale community: \"People are more tech-proficient than we thought.\"\nAnd besides, where else would he learn to play mahjong?", "52": "The market for products designed specifically for older adults could reach $30 billion by next year, and startups(初创公司) want in on the action. What they sometimes lack is feedback from the people who they hope will use their products. So Brookdale, the country's largest owner of retirement communities, has been inviting a few select entrepreneurs just to move in for a few days, show off their products and hear what the residents have to say.\nThat's what brought Dayle Rodriguez, 28, all the way from England to the dining room of Brookdale South Bay in Torrance, California. Rodriguez is the community and marketing manager for a company called Sentab. The startup's product, SentabTV, enables older adults who may not be comfortable with computers to access email, video chat and social media using just their televisions and a remote control.\n\"It's nothing new, it's nothing too complicated and it's natural because lots of people have TV remotes,\" says Rodriguez.\nBut none of that is the topic of conversation in the Brookdale dining room. Instead, Rodriguez solicits residents' advice on what he should get on his cheeseburger and how he should spend the afternoon. Playing cards was on the agenda, as well as learning to play mahjong(麻将).\nRodriguez says it's important that residents here don't feel like he's selling them something. \"I've had more feedback in a passive approach,\" he says. \"Playing pool, playing cards, having dinner, having lunch,\" all work better \"than going through a survey of questions. When they get to know me and to trust me, knowing for sure I'm not selling them something—there'll be more honest feedback from them.\"\nRodriguez is just the seventh entrepreneur to move into one of Brookdale's 1,100 senior living communities. Other new products in the program have included a kind of full-body blow dryer and specially designed clothing that allows people with disabilities to dress and undress themselves.\nMary Lou Busch, 93, agreed to try the Sentab system. She tells Rodriguez that it might be good for someone, but not for her. \"I have the computer and FaceTime, which I talk with my family on,\" she explains. She also has an iPad and a smartphone. \"So I do pretty much everything I need to do.\"\nTo be fair, if Rodriguez had wanted feedback from some more technophobic(害怕技术的) seniors, he might have ended up in the wrong Brookdale community. This one is located in the heart of Southern California's aerospace corridor. Many residents have backgrounds in engineering, business and academic circles.\nBut Rodriguez says he's still learning something important by moving into this Brookdale community: \"People are more tech-proficient than we thought.\"\nAnd besides, where else would he learn to play mahjong?", "53": "The market for products designed specifically for older adults could reach $30 billion by next year, and startups(初创公司) want in on the action. What they sometimes lack is feedback from the people who they hope will use their products. So Brookdale, the country's largest owner of retirement communities, has been inviting a few select entrepreneurs just to move in for a few days, show off their products and hear what the residents have to say.\nThat's what brought Dayle Rodriguez, 28, all the way from England to the dining room of Brookdale South Bay in Torrance, California. Rodriguez is the community and marketing manager for a company called Sentab. The startup's product, SentabTV, enables older adults who may not be comfortable with computers to access email, video chat and social media using just their televisions and a remote control.\n\"It's nothing new, it's nothing too complicated and it's natural because lots of people have TV remotes,\" says Rodriguez.\nBut none of that is the topic of conversation in the Brookdale dining room. Instead, Rodriguez solicits residents' advice on what he should get on his cheeseburger and how he should spend the afternoon. Playing cards was on the agenda, as well as learning to play mahjong(麻将).\nRodriguez says it's important that residents here don't feel like he's selling them something. \"I've had more feedback in a passive approach,\" he says. \"Playing pool, playing cards, having dinner, having lunch,\" all work better \"than going through a survey of questions. When they get to know me and to trust me, knowing for sure I'm not selling them something—there'll be more honest feedback from them.\"\nRodriguez is just the seventh entrepreneur to move into one of Brookdale's 1,100 senior living communities. Other new products in the program have included a kind of full-body blow dryer and specially designed clothing that allows people with disabilities to dress and undress themselves.\nMary Lou Busch, 93, agreed to try the Sentab system. She tells Rodriguez that it might be good for someone, but not for her. \"I have the computer and FaceTime, which I talk with my family on,\" she explains. She also has an iPad and a smartphone. \"So I do pretty much everything I need to do.\"\nTo be fair, if Rodriguez had wanted feedback from some more technophobic(害怕技术的) seniors, he might have ended up in the wrong Brookdale community. This one is located in the heart of Southern California's aerospace corridor. Many residents have backgrounds in engineering, business and academic circles.\nBut Rodriguez says he's still learning something important by moving into this Brookdale community: \"People are more tech-proficient than we thought.\"\nAnd besides, where else would he learn to play mahjong?", "54": "The market for products designed specifically for older adults could reach $30 billion by next year, and startups(初创公司) want in on the action. What they sometimes lack is feedback from the people who they hope will use their products. So Brookdale, the country's largest owner of retirement communities, has been inviting a few select entrepreneurs just to move in for a few days, show off their products and hear what the residents have to say.\nThat's what brought Dayle Rodriguez, 28, all the way from England to the dining room of Brookdale South Bay in Torrance, California. Rodriguez is the community and marketing manager for a company called Sentab. The startup's product, SentabTV, enables older adults who may not be comfortable with computers to access email, video chat and social media using just their televisions and a remote control.\n\"It's nothing new, it's nothing too complicated and it's natural because lots of people have TV remotes,\" says Rodriguez.\nBut none of that is the topic of conversation in the Brookdale dining room. Instead, Rodriguez solicits residents' advice on what he should get on his cheeseburger and how he should spend the afternoon. Playing cards was on the agenda, as well as learning to play mahjong(麻将).\nRodriguez says it's important that residents here don't feel like he's selling them something. \"I've had more feedback in a passive approach,\" he says. \"Playing pool, playing cards, having dinner, having lunch,\" all work better \"than going through a survey of questions. When they get to know me and to trust me, knowing for sure I'm not selling them something—there'll be more honest feedback from them.\"\nRodriguez is just the seventh entrepreneur to move into one of Brookdale's 1,100 senior living communities. Other new products in the program have included a kind of full-body blow dryer and specially designed clothing that allows people with disabilities to dress and undress themselves.\nMary Lou Busch, 93, agreed to try the Sentab system. She tells Rodriguez that it might be good for someone, but not for her. \"I have the computer and FaceTime, which I talk with my family on,\" she explains. She also has an iPad and a smartphone. \"So I do pretty much everything I need to do.\"\nTo be fair, if Rodriguez had wanted feedback from some more technophobic(害怕技术的) seniors, he might have ended up in the wrong Brookdale community. This one is located in the heart of Southern California's aerospace corridor. Many residents have backgrounds in engineering, business and academic circles.\nBut Rodriguez says he's still learning something important by moving into this Brookdale community: \"People are more tech-proficient than we thought.\"\nAnd besides, where else would he learn to play mahjong?", "55": "The market for products designed specifically for older adults could reach $30 billion by next year, and startups(初创公司) want in on the action. What they sometimes lack is feedback from the people who they hope will use their products. So Brookdale, the country's largest owner of retirement communities, has been inviting a few select entrepreneurs just to move in for a few days, show off their products and hear what the residents have to say.\nThat's what brought Dayle Rodriguez, 28, all the way from England to the dining room of Brookdale South Bay in Torrance, California. Rodriguez is the community and marketing manager for a company called Sentab. The startup's product, SentabTV, enables older adults who may not be comfortable with computers to access email, video chat and social media using just their televisions and a remote control.\n\"It's nothing new, it's nothing too complicated and it's natural because lots of people have TV remotes,\" says Rodriguez.\nBut none of that is the topic of conversation in the Brookdale dining room. Instead, Rodriguez solicits residents' advice on what he should get on his cheeseburger and how he should spend the afternoon. Playing cards was on the agenda, as well as learning to play mahjong(麻将).\nRodriguez says it's important that residents here don't feel like he's selling them something. \"I've had more feedback in a passive approach,\" he says. \"Playing pool, playing cards, having dinner, having lunch,\" all work better \"than going through a survey of questions. When they get to know me and to trust me, knowing for sure I'm not selling them something—there'll be more honest feedback from them.\"\nRodriguez is just the seventh entrepreneur to move into one of Brookdale's 1,100 senior living communities. Other new products in the program have included a kind of full-body blow dryer and specially designed clothing that allows people with disabilities to dress and undress themselves.\nMary Lou Busch, 93, agreed to try the Sentab system. She tells Rodriguez that it might be good for someone, but not for her. \"I have the computer and FaceTime, which I talk with my family on,\" she explains. She also has an iPad and a smartphone. \"So I do pretty much everything I need to do.\"\nTo be fair, if Rodriguez had wanted feedback from some more technophobic(害怕技术的) seniors, he might have ended up in the wrong Brookdale community. This one is located in the heart of Southern California's aerospace corridor. Many residents have backgrounds in engineering, business and academic circles.\nBut Rodriguez says he's still learning something important by moving into this Brookdale community: \"People are more tech-proficient than we thought.\"\nAnd besides, where else would he learn to play mahjong?"}, "questions": [{"id": "2019-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What did Cathy and Brenda finish doing last week?", "options": ["A) A six-month-long negotiation.", "B) Preparations for the party.", "C) A project with a troublesome client.", "D) Gift wrapping for the colleagues."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A project with a troublesome client.\n【定位】We just wrapped up a project with a difficult client last week.\n我们上周刚刚搞定了一个难缠客户的项目。\n【信号】完成信号 wrapped up 加时间 last week。We just wrapped up a project with a\ndifficult client last week. 里 wrapped up 是『完成、收尾』的强信号，又精确带上 last\nweek，与题干时间对上，这一句就是答案句。\n【替换】wrapped up a project with a difficult client ↔ A project with a troublesome client\n同义改写 difficult=troublesome\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 A six-month-long negotiation. 里的 six-month 看似呼应原文 We've only been\nworking together the last six months，但 six months 说的是两人共事的时长，不是上周\n做完的事，更没有 negotiation（谈判）一词，是把听到的数字硬安到本题上的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：We've only been working together the last six months\nB) 原词陷阱\nPreparations for the party. 借了原文 she can focus on wedding preparations 里的\npreparations，但原文是说 Brenda 接下来要忙婚礼筹备，并非上周已完成的事，时间和归\n属都不对。\n借⽤原⽂：she can focus on wedding preparations\nD) 原词陷阱\nGift wrapping for the colleagues. 利用了原文 wrapped up 的 wrap 字面，制造『包装礼\n物 gift wrapping』的联想，但录音从未提到给同事包装礼物，纯属字面误导。\n借⽤原⽂：We just wrapped up a project", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What is John going to do for Brenda?", "options": ["A) Take wedding photos.", "B) Advertise her company.", "C) Start a small business.", "D) Throw a celebration party."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Take wedding photos.\n【定位】I'll actually be taking photos during the big event as a wedding gift.\n婚礼当天我其实会负责拍照，作为送给新人的结婚礼物。\n【信号】将来式 I'll be 自述要做的事。I'll actually be taking photos during the big event as a\nwedding gift 用将来进行时 I'll be taking 由 John 本人说出自己届时要做的事，是『某人\n打算做什么』的直接应答信号。\n【替换】taking photos during the big event ↔ Take wedding photos\n概括替换 the big event=wedding\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nAdvertise her company. 借了原文 I worked in advertising for a while，但那是 John 大\n学毕业后的旧工作，且 advertising 是行业、不是为 Brenda 做广告，时间与对象都错。\n借⽤原⽂：I worked in advertising for a while\nC) 原词陷阱\nStart a small business. 来自 I turned my photography hobby into a small business，\n但那是 John 已经做成的事、是他自己的事业，不是他要『为 Brenda』做的，张冠了对\n象。\n借⽤原⽂：I turned my photography hobby into a small business\nD) 未提及\nThrow a celebration party. 借派对场景做联想，但录音里没有任何人说要为 Brenda 办庆\n祝派对，John 要做的是拍照，纯属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "How did Cathy feel when asked to bake the cake?", "options": ["A) Hesitant.", "B) Nervous.", "C) Flattered.", "D) Surprised."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） Nervous.\n【定位】I was a bit nervous saying yes, because I'm far from a professional.\n我答应的时候有点紧张，因为我离专业水准还差得远。\n【信号】情绪自陈 a bit nervous 加原因。I was a bit nervous saying yes, because I'm far from\na professional 里 Cathy 直接说出 nervous，并用 because 解释紧张原因，是态度的直接\n自陈信号。\n【替换】I was a bit nervous ↔ Nervous\n原词直陈即态度\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nHesitant. 表犹豫，与紧张方向相近易混，但 Cathy 是 nervous saying yes——她最终答\n应了，并未表现出迟疑不决，原文没有犹豫的依据。\n借⽤原⽂：I was a bit nervous saying yes\nC) 未提及\nFlattered. 指受到抬举而高兴，但原文 because I'm far from a professional 透出的是担心\n自己不够专业，并无被恭维的得意之情，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nSurprised. 借了原文 I was a little surprised to be invited，但 surprised 说的是 Cathy\n对『被邀请参加派对』感到意外，不是对『被请求做蛋糕』的感受，被问的那件事换了，情\n绪原词便对不上本题。\n借⽤原⽂：I was a little surprised to be invited", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man suggest the woman do?", "options": ["A) Start her own bakery.", "B) Improve her baking skill.", "C) Share her cooking experience.", "D) Prepare for the wedding"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Start her own bakery.\n【定位】with your event planning experience, you could very well open your own shop.\n凭你的活动策划经验，你完全可以开一家属于自己的店。\n【信号】建议句式 you could very well。you could very well open your own shop 用 you\ncould 这一典型建议句式，由男方对女方说出，是『建议做什么』的直接信号。\n【替换】open your own shop ↔ Start her own bakery\n具体化替换 shop=bakery\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nImprove her baking skill. 围绕 baking 做合理联想，但男方夸她 You're a natural 已是天\n赋型选手，并未建议她去提升手艺，原文没有这层意思。\nC) 原词陷阱\nShare her cooking experience. 借了原文 with your event planning experience 的\nexperience，但原文是说凭经验去开店，并非建议她去『分享』经验，动作被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：with your event planning experience\nD) 原词陷阱\nPrepare for the wedding 利用全程的婚礼话题，但男方的建议是开店，并非让她去筹备婚\n礼；筹备婚礼是背景事件，不是男方提出的建议，属未提及的建议。\n借⽤原⽂：with your event planning experience, you could very well open your own shop", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man say about college students as compared with high schoolers?", "options": ["A) They have to spend more time studying.", "B) They have to participate in club activities.", "C) They have to be more responsible for what they do.", "D) They have to choose a specific academic discipline."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They have to be more responsible for what they do.\n【定位】Many aspects are different here at university. The most important one is that you have to take\nmore individual responsibility for your actions.\n大学里很多方面都不一样。其中最重要的一点是，你必须为自己的行为承担更多的个人\n责任。\n【信号】最高级 the most important 点睛。男方先说 Many aspects are different，紧接着用最\n高级 The most important one is 把众多不同里\"最重要的\"那条单拎出来——最高级之后就\n是答案句，他要强调的正是\"承担更多个人责任\"。\n【替换】take more individual responsibility for your actions ↔ be more responsible for what they do\n名词转形容词的同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里反复出现 study（how much effort you put into study），A 项 spend more\ntime studying 正是盗用这个原词；但原文说的是\"投入多少由你的自律决定\"，并非\"必须花\n更多时间学习\"，听到 study 就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：how much effort you put into study\nB) 答非所问\nB 项 participate in club activities 里的 club activities 出现在后文第 9 段，是第 8 题讨论\n的内容，与第 5 题\"最重要的不同\"无关；且原文是说\"不该花太多时间在社团\"，更非\"必须参\n加\"。\n借⽤原⽂：there are a lot of wonderful clubs\nD) 未提及\nD 项 choose a specific academic discipline（选一门具体专业）听上去很像大学场景该有\n的事，但录音从头到尾没说要\"选专业\"，属凭常识脑补、原文无据。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What are college students expected to do according to the man?", "options": ["A) Get ready for a career.", "B) Make a lot of friends.", "C) Set a long-term goal.", "D) Behave like adults."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Behave like adults.\n【定位】It's just that suddenly at university you are expected to behave like an adult, that\nmeans concentrating on the direction of your life in general and your own academic\nperformance specifically.\n只是说，到了大学你突然就被期望像个成年人一样行事，也就是说，总体上要专注于自己人\n生的方向，具体来说要专注于自己的学业表现。\n【信号】被问者应答里的 expected to。女方追问\"Nobody cares, you mean?\"，男方的应答里出\n现 you are expected to behave like an adult，正用题干同款词 expected to——被问者\n的直接应答 + 题干原词双重信号，锁定此句。\n【替换】behave like an adult ↔ Behave like adults.\n近乎原句的同义对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 Get ready for a career. 是大学常被联想的目标，但录音里男方谈的是\"像成年人一样\n行事、专注人生方向和学业\"，并没说\"为就业做准备\"，属常识脑补。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音后文确有 You will make interesting friends，B 项 Make a lot of friends. 盗用了\nfriends；但那是第 7 题讲交友、且原文强调\"只留尊重学业的朋友\"，并非\"被期望多交朋\n友\"。\n借⽤原⽂：You will make interesting friends\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说 concentrating on the direction of your life（专注人生方向），C 项 Set a long-\nterm goal.（设定长期目标）把\"方向\"偷换成\"设目标\"，看似相近实则不是一回事，原文没\n说要\"设定目标\"。\n借⽤原⽂：concentrating on the direction of your life", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What kind of friends does the man suggest the woman make as a college student?", "options": ["A) Those who share her academic interests.", "B) Those who respect her student commitments.", "C) Those who can help her when she is in need.", "D) Those who go to the same clubs as she does."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Those who respect her student commitments.\n【定位】You will make interesting friends, but you need only keep the friends who respect your\nstudent commitments.\n你会结识有趣的朋友，但你只需保留那些尊重你学生本分的朋友。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是真意。前半 You will make interesting friends 是铺垫，转折词 but 之\n后 keep the friends who respect your student commitments 才是男方真正的建议——\nbut 后是答案句，前半句是用来干扰\"听到 friends 就选\"的人。\n【替换】keep the friends who respect your student commitments ↔\nThose who respect her student commitments.\n人称转换的同义对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 share her academic interests 把\"学业\"和\"兴趣\"拼在一起，听上去合理，但录音说的\n是\"尊重你学生本分\"，从未提\"志趣相投\"，属脑补。\nC) 未提及\nC 项 help her when she is in need（在她需要时能帮她的人）是对\"好朋友\"的常识定义，\n但录音的标准是\"尊重学业\"，与\"能帮忙\"无关，原文无据。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音后句确有 there are a lot of wonderful clubs，D 项 go to the same clubs as she\ndoes 盗用 clubs；但 clubs 是讲社团时间分配（第 8 题）的，男方从没说\"和同社团的人交\n友\"，听到 clubs 就选正中陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：there are a lot of wonderful clubs", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What kind of club activities should college students engage in according to the man?", "options": ["A) Those helpful for tapping their potential.", "B) Those conducive to improving their social skills.", "C) Those helpful for cultivating individual interests.", "D) Those conducive to their academic studies."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Those conducive to their academic studies.\n【定位】Also, there are a lot of wonderful clubs, but you shouldn't allocate too much time to club\nactivities, unless they are directly related to your study.\n另外，社团很多很棒，但你不该在社团活动上分配太多时间，除非它们与你的学业直接\n相关。\n【信号】unless 引出唯一例外。男方先说\"不该花太多时间在社团\"，再用 unless they are directly\nrelated to your study 给出唯一例外——unless 后的条件就是\"什么样的社团可以参加\"，\n是答案句的灵魂。\n【替换】directly related to your study ↔ Those conducive to their academic studies.\nrelated to=conducive to 的同义改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 helpful for tapping their potential（有助发掘潜能）是社团活动常被说的好处，听上\n去合理，但录音的唯一标准是\"与学业相关\"，未提潜能，属脑补。\nB) 未提及\nB 项 conducive to improving their social skills（有助提升社交能力）也是社团的常见联\n想好处，但录音只说\"与学业相关\"才值得花时间，没提社交，原文无据。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 helpful for cultivating individual interests（有助培养个人兴趣）与 D 的\"学业相\n关\"形成正面对立；录音明说标准是\"与学业相关\"，C 把\"学业\"偷换成\"个人兴趣\"，方向相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：directly related to your study", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the speaker say about most successful people?", "options": ["A) They break away from traditional ways of thinking.", "B) They are prepared to work harder than anyone else.", "C) They are good at refining old formulas.", "D) They bring their potential into full play."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They break away from traditional ways of thinking.\n【定位】Most successful people are unorthodox persons whose minds wander outside traditional ways\nof thinking. Instead of trying to refine old formulas, they invent new ones.\n大多数成功者都是不循常规的人，他们的思维游走于传统思维方式之外。他们不去改良旧公\n式，而是发明新公式。\n【信号】总括句+Instead of 取舍信号。录音开篇就是对成功者的总括，并用 Instead of trying to\nrefine old formulas, they invent new ones 中的 Instead of 给出明确取舍——'不是改良\n旧的、而是发明新的'，这个'不是A而是B'的信号直接点出成功者的本质特征，就是答案句。\n【替换】minds wander outside traditional ways of thinking ↔\nbreak away from traditional ways of thinking\nwander outside ≈ break away from（跳出传统）\nthey invent new ones ↔ break away from traditional ways of thinking\n'发明新的'佐证'打破传统'\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThey are prepared to work harder than anyone else. 的措辞几乎照搬第 2 段 he was\nprepared to work harder than anyone else，听到原话极易选；但那句讲的是 Killy 个人\n一开始的做法，且后文紧接 simply training harder would never be enough（光苦练远\n远不够），说明'更努力'恰恰不是成功者的关键共性，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：he was prepared to work harder than anyone else\nC) 与原文相反\nThey are good at refining old formulas. 直接挪用录音 Instead of trying to refine old\nformulas 里的 refine old formulas；但原文是用 Instead of 把'改良旧公式'否定掉、说成\n功者偏偏不这么做，选项却说他们'擅长改良旧公式'，正好与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Instead of trying to refine old formulas\nD) 未提及\nThey bring their potential into full play. 听起来像成功学套话，但录音通篇讲的是'跳出传\n统、发明新法'，从没提到'发挥潜能（potential）'这层意思，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the speaker say about Killy's experiments?", "options": ["A) They contributed to the popularity of skiing worldwide.", "B) They resulted in a brand-new style of skiing techniques.", "C) They promoted the scientific use of skiing poles.", "D) They made explosive news in the sports world."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They resulted in a brand-new style of skiing techniques.\n【定位】His experiments resulted in a new style that was almost exactly opposite the accepted\ntechnique of the time.\n他的实验催生了一种全新风格，几乎与当时公认的技术完全相反。\n【信号】因果信号 resulted in 直引出结果。录音 His experiments resulted in a new style 里的\nresulted in 是明确的因果信号词，experiments 是因、紧跟的 a new style 就是要找的\n果，信号词后面的内容直接就是答案句。\n【替换】resulted in a new style ↔ resulted in a brand-new style of skiing techniques\nnew style ≈ brand-new style of skiing techniques\nalmost exactly opposite the accepted technique of the time ↔\na brand-new style of skiing techniques\n'与公认技术相反'强化了'全新'\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nThey contributed to the popularity of skiing worldwide. 把实验的影响夸大为'让滑雪在\n全世界流行'；录音只说实验催生了新风格、帮 Killy 自己缩短了比赛时间，从未说它让滑雪\n运动在全球流行，属过度推断/放大范围。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey promoted the scientific use of skiing poles. 挪用了录音 He also used ski poles\nin an unorthodox way 里的 ski poles；但原文说他是'用非常规的方式（unorthodox\nway）'用雪杖来助推，根本不是'科学使用（scientific use）'，把'非常规'偷换成了'科学'。\n借⽤原⽂：He also used ski poles in an unorthodox way\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey made explosive news in the sports world. 抓住了录音里很扎眼的 explosive 一\n词；但原文 The explosive new style helped cut Killy's racing time 里 explosive 修饰的\n是'new style（爆发式的新风格）'，讲它大幅缩短了比赛时间，根本没说'造成爆炸性新\n闻'，原词被挪了搭配。\n借⽤原⽂：The explosive new style helped cut Killy's racing time", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What is said to be Killy's biggest honor in his skiing career?", "options": ["A) He was recognized as a genius in the world of sports.", "B) He competed in all major skiing events in the world.", "C) He won three gold medals in one Winter Olympics.", "D) He broke three world skiing records in three years."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He won three gold medals in one Winter Olympics.\n【定位】The next year he won three gold medals in the Winter Olympics, a record in ski racing that\nhas never been topped.\n第二年他在冬奥会上赢得三枚金牌，这是滑雪比赛中一项从未被超越的纪录。\n【信号】数字+最高级 never been topped。录音 he won three gold medals in the Winter\nOlympics, a record in ski racing that has never been topped 同时给了硬数字（three\ngold medals）和最高级评价（never been topped 从未被超越），'从未被超越'正是'最大\n荣誉'的信号，定为答案句。\n【替换】he won three gold medals in the Winter Olympics ↔\nHe won three gold medals in one Winter Olympics.\n几乎原句对应，only 'the'→'one'\na record in ski racing that has never been topped ↔\nHe won three gold medals in one Winter Olympics.\n'从未被超越的纪录'印证这是最大荣誉\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nHe was recognized as a genius in the world of sports. 利用了第 4 段 innovations\ndon't require genius 里的 genius；但原文恰恰说'创新不需要天才'，是在否定'天才'这一\n前提，选项却说他'被公认为天才'，与原文意思相反，且这不是在讲荣誉。\n借⽤原⽂：innovations don't require genius\nB) 偷换概念\nHe competed in all major skiing events in the world. 听上去呼应录音 he captured\nvirtually every major skiing trophy；但原文说的是'夺得几乎每一项重大滑雪奖杯\n（captured trophy）'，是'拿奖'不是'参加（competed in）所有赛事'，把'夺奖'偷换成'参\n赛'，且 all 比原文 virtually every 更绝对。\n借⽤原⽂：he captured virtually every major skiing trophy\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe broke three world skiing records in three years. 抓住录音里的数字 three 与年份信\n息（1966、1967、the next year）东拼西凑出'三年破三项世界纪录'；但原文是 In 1966\nand 1967 he captured virtually every major skiing trophy 讲夺奖、次年 he won three\ngold medals 讲三金，'three'配的是金牌不是纪录，更没说'破三项世界纪录'，数字张冠李\n戴。\n借⽤原⽂：he won three gold medals in the Winter Olympics", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What happens to guinea pigs, when blood samples of angry people are injected into them?", "options": ["A) They appear restless.", "B) They lose consciousness.", "C) They become upset.", "D) They die almost instantly."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They die almost instantly.\n【定位】Blood samples taken from persons experiencing intense fear or anger when injected into\nguinea pigs have killed them in less than two minutes.\n从经历强烈恐惧或愤怒的人身上抽取的血样，注入豚鼠体内后，在不到两分钟内就把它们杀\n死了。\n【信号】结果动词 killed 即结局落点。这句出现强结果动词 killed them in less than two\nminutes，killed 直接交代了豚鼠的最终结局，是承载答案的关键动作词，听到它就锁定这\n一句。\n【替换】killed them in less than two minutes ↔ They die almost instantly.\n致死改写为致死，时长改写为副词\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey appear restless. 说豚鼠\"显得不安\"，远轻于录音的 killed，把致命结局偷换成轻微反\n应；录音从未提及豚鼠\"不安\"，属程度被改轻的无中生有。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey lose consciousness.（失去意识）听上去与\"被注射后出事\"沾边，但录音明说的是\nkilled them（被杀死），死亡比昏迷重一档；选项偷偷把\"死\"降成\"昏迷\"。\n借⽤原⽂：killed them in less than two minutes\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey become upset. 里的 upset 正是录音里反复出现的原词 Emotional upsets，但那是\n形容\"人的情绪波动\"产生毒素，不是说豚鼠\"变得难受\"；选项盗用 upset 制造\"听到就想\n选\"的错觉。\n借⽤原⽂：Emotional upsets generate powerful and deadly toxic substances", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say about every thought you have?", "options": ["A) It has an instant effect on your body chemistry.", "B) It keeps returning to you every now and then.", "C) It leaves you with a long lasting impression.", "D) It contributes to the shaping of your mind."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has an instant effect on your body chemistry.\n【定位】Every thought that you have affects your body chemistry within a split second.\n你产生的每一个念头，都会在一瞬间影响你的身体化学反应。\n【信号】时间词 within a split second 即时效落点。题干对象 thought 一出现，紧跟的判定就是\naffects your body chemistry within a split second，其中\"a split second（一瞬间）\"正\n是预读锁定的时效维度落点，听到它即定位本句。\n【替换】affects your body chemistry within a split second ↔\nIt has an instant effect on your body chemistry.\n动词改名词、瞬间换同义词\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt keeps returning to you every now and then. 说念头\"时不时回来\"，与录音强调的\"瞬\n间作用于身体\"无关，录音没说念头会反复回返，属无中生有。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt leaves you with a long lasting impression. 的\"long lasting（持久）\"恰与录音的\nwithin a split second（瞬间）在时间维度上相反；选项取了与原文相反的极性。\n借⽤原⽂：within a split second\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt contributes to the shaping of your mind. 谈的是\"塑造心智\"，方向是\"心智被塑\"；而\n本段说的是念头作用于 body chemistry（身体），作用对象被掉了个；\"塑造\"相关内容其\n实在后文谈 subconscious 时才出现，安到本题即张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：how our subconscious mind shapes our health", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the speaker say about every thought you have?", "options": ["A) To succeed while feeling irritated.", "B) To feel happy without good health.", "C) To be free from frustration and failure.", "D) To enjoy good health while in dark moods."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To enjoy good health while in dark moods.\n【定位】It is impossible to be fearful, anxious, irritated, and healthy at the same time. It is not just\ndifficult, it is impossible.\n一个人不可能在恐惧、焦虑、烦躁的同时又保持健康。这不只是困难，而是根本不可能。\n【信号】强判定词 impossible 加重复强调。这句以 It is impossible 作判定，并用 It is not just\ndifficult, it is impossible 重复加重语气，强调正是答案句的信号——说话人反复强调处即\n考点。\n【替换】impossible to be fearful, anxious, irritated, and healthy at the same time ↔\nTo enjoy good health while in dark moods.\n负面情绪概括为 dark moods，并存关系保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo succeed while feeling irritated. 里 irritated 取自原文 irritated，但 succeed（成功）\n原文从未提及；选项盗用 irritated 一词，再拼上无中生有的\"成功\"。\n借⽤原⽂：fearful, anxious, irritated\nB) 偷换概念\nTo feel happy without good health. 谈\"没有健康却快乐\"，方向与原文\"坏情绪 vs 健康不\n能并存\"对不上：原文说的是\"坏情绪伴健康不可能\"，不是\"无健康却快乐\"，逻辑被掉换。\nC) 原词陷阱\nTo be free from frustration and failure. 里 frustration 取自原文 frustration，但\"摆脱挫\n折与失败\"是录音没有下过的判断，选项盗用 frustration 制造熟悉感。\n借⽤原⽂：fear, anger, frustration, and stress produce", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the passage say about our mind and body?", "options": ["A) They are closely connected.", "B) They function in a similar way.", "C) They are too complex to understand.", "D) They are too complex to understand."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are closely connected.\n【定位】The mind-body connection is such that if, for example, we want to avoid something, very\noften our subconscious mind will arrange it.\n心身之间的关联是这样的：比如当我们想回避某件事时，潜意识常常会替我们把它安排\n出来。\n【信号】关系名词 connection 直接点题。这句以 The mind-body connection 把心智与身体直接\n捆在一起命名为\"关联\"，connection 就是两者关系的总括落点；本段 your body's health\nis a reflection of your mental health 亦呼应同一结论。\n【替换】The mind-body connection ↔ They are closely connected.\n名词 connection 还原为形容词 connected\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nThey function in a similar way. 说两者\"运作方式相似\"，而录音说的是\"相互关联\n（connection）\"，相连不等于运作相似；选项把\"关联\"偷换成\"相似\"这种另一种关系。\nC) 未提及\nThey are too complex to understand. 说两者\"复杂到难以理解\"，但录音通篇在阐明两者\n如何相连、还说 we are halfway to doing something about them（我们已离解决问题不\n远），态度是\"可认识、可处理\"，并非\"难懂\"，属无中生有且基调相反。\nD) 未提及\nThey are too complex to understand. 与 C 内容完全相同，同样把两者说成\"复杂难懂\"，\n与录音\"相连且可处理\"的结论不符，理由同 C。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about beginners and expert pianists?", "options": ["A) They differ in their appreciation of music.", "B) They focus their attention on different things.", "C) They finger the piano keys in different ways.", "D) They choose different pieces of music to play."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They focus their attention on different things.\n【定位】They have to give so much thought and attention to the low-level mechanics of handling the\nball or fingering the keys or reading the music, that they are unable to give any thought to the\nthing that matters—the game, or the music, respectively. With experts, it's just the other way\naround. They're open to the tactical possibilities and the musical challenges precisely because\nthey're freed, through skill, from the need to pay attention to the low-level details.\n新手必须把大量心思和注意力放在按球、按琴键、读谱这些低层次的机械动作上，以至于无\n暇去想真正要紧的东西——比赛本身或音乐本身。而专家恰恰相反：正因为靠技能从关注低\n层细节中解放出来，他们能把心思放在战术可能性与音乐挑战上。\n【信号】对比信号 the other way around。新手那句强调注意力在 the low-level mechanics，紧\n接着第 2 段 With experts, it's just the other way around（恰恰相反）这一对比信号点明\n两类人注意力放的位置正相反，正反对照处就是答案句。\n【替换】give so much thought and attention to the low-level mechanics ↔\nfocus their attention on different things\n\"注意力放在低层机械\"被概括为\"注意力放在不同东西上\"\nit's just the other way around ↔ different things\n\"正相反\"对应\"不同\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了录音反复出现的 music，但录音说的是两类人\"注意力落点\"不同，从没说他们对音乐\n的 appreciation（欣赏/鉴赏）不同，是借 music 一词答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：reading the music\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 抓住录音里很具体的 fingering the keys，但原文是说新手\"得分心去 finger the keys\"、\n专家不必，讲的是注意力，并非说两人\"指法方式不同\"，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：fingering the keys\nD) 未提及\nD 用了 pieces of music/play，但录音通篇没提两类人\"选不同曲子弹\"，纯属拿熟词拼出的\n未提及内容。\n借⽤原⽂：the music", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do smart basketball players do according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) They manage to cooperate well with their teammates.", "B) They use effective tactics to defeat their competitors.", "C) They try hard to meet the spectators' expectations.", "D) They attach great importance to high performance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They attach great importance to high performance.\n【定位】Smart basketball players or skilled musicians need to pay close attention to the demands of\nhigh performance, to the challenges to be overcome.\n聪明的篮球运动员或熟练的音乐家需要密切关注高水平表现的要求，关注那些需要克服的\n挑战。\n【信号】主语直击信号 Smart basketball players。第 4 段开头直接以 Smart basketball players\n为主语，谓语 need to pay close attention to the demands of high performance，主\n语与题干完全对上，是直击答案句。\n【替换】pay close attention to the demands of high performance ↔\nattach great importance to high performance\n\"密切关注\"=\"高度重视\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 的 cooperate well with their teammates 听着很合\"打篮球要配合\"的常识，但录音从头\n到尾没提队友配合，是用生活经验冒充原文、未提及。\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 借了录音里的\"战术\"概念（tactical），但原文 tactical possibilities 讲的是专家\"对战术\n保持开放\"，并没说他们\"用战术击败对手\"，是偷换为打败对手的未提及内容。\n借⽤原⽂：the tactical possibilities\nC) 未提及\nC 的 meet the spectators' expectations（满足观众期待）录音里完全没出现，连相关词\n都没有，纯属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do we learn about the new study published in an online video?", "options": ["A) It marks a breakthrough in behavioral science.", "B) It adopts a conventional approach to research.", "C) It supports a piece of conventional wisdom.", "D) It gives rise to controversy among experts."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It supports a piece of conventional wisdom.\n【定位】This is not a surprising finding. It supports what we might almost think of as\nconventional wisdom.\n这并不是一个令人惊讶的发现。它印证了我们几乎可以视为常识的东西。\n【信号】定性信号 supports…conventional wisdom。第 7 段先用 not a surprising finding 定\n调\"不意外\"，再直接说 It supports what we might almost think of as conventional\nwisdom，supports + conventional wisdom 就是对研究的定性，是答案句。\n【替换】It supports what we might almost think of as conventional wisdom ↔\nIt supports a piece of conventional wisdom\n近乎原句，只把从句压成名词短语\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说这是 breakthrough（突破），但录音明确说 not a surprising finding（并不令人惊\n讶），\"不意外\"与\"突破\"正相反，属与原文相反兼过度拔高。\n借⽤原⽂：This is not a surprising finding\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说研究\"用了常规/老套方法\"，但录音说它用的是 a new study using an eye-tracking\ndevice（眼动仪），是新手段而非 conventional approach，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：a new study using an eye-tracking device\nD) 未提及\nD 说研究\"在专家中引发争议\"，但录音对该发现的定性是\"不意外、印证常识\"，根本没提任\n何争议，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What overwhelms the speaker when she buys her summer outfits each year?", "options": ["A) People's envy of slim models.", "B) People's craze for good health.", "C) The increasing range of fancy products.", "D) The great variety of slimming products."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The great variety of slimming products.\n【定位】I'm overwhelmed with a wide range of different slimming products each year.\n每年我都被五花八门、种类繁多的减肥瘦身产品弄得不知所措。\n【信号】题干原词 overwhelmed 在录音中直接落点。录音 I'm overwhelmed with a wide range\nof different slimming products 里的 overwhelmed 正是题干的提问词，这种\"题干提问\n词在原文原样出现\"的句子就是答案句的强信号，紧跟其后的 a wide range of different\nslimming products 就是答案对象。\n【替换】a wide range of different slimming products ↔ The great variety of slimming products\na wide range of different = The great variety of\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音末句确实提到 very slim models，A 项 People's envy of slim models. 借这个 slim\nmodels 引诱你\"听到就选\"，但录音只说产品由瘦模特代言，从未说人们\"羡慕(envy)\"模\n特，envy 是无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：very slim models\nB) 偷换概念\n录音通篇讲减肥产品，结尾才提 Eat in a balanced way 的健康主张，但 19 题问的是\"买夏\n装时被什么震到\"，B 项 People's craze for good health. 既不在答案句、又把对象偷换\n成\"健康狂热\"，答非所问。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 The increasing range of fancy products. 与正确项句式几乎一样（都说\"产品种类\n多\"），但把 slimming 换成了 fancy（花哨的）。录音说的是 slimming products（减肥\n产品），从未说 fancy products，这一字之差就是错点。\n借⽤原⽂：slimming products", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the speaker think of girls wrapping their legs up with slimming tape?", "options": ["A) They appear vigorous.", "B) They appear strange.", "C) They look charming.", "D) They look unhealthy."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They appear strange.\n【定位】In Hong Kong, I've seen girls wrapping their whole body or both legs up with a special type\nof slimming tape which is supposed to help make them thinner. But it just reminded me of the\nroasted ham my mother usually puts on the dinner table at Christmas.\n在香港，我见过女孩们用一种据说能让人变瘦的特制塑身胶带把全身或双腿缠起来。但这只\n让我联想到我母亲圣诞节常端上餐桌的烤火腿。\n【信号】转折 But 后的比喻才是真态度。前半句客观描述\"用胶带缠腿\"，But it just reminded me\nof the roasted ham 用 But 转折，后面把缠腿的样子比作\"烤火腿\"，转折后的这个比喻才\n暴露说话人的真实评价——觉得样子怪异、滑稽。\n【替换】it just reminded me of the roasted ham ↔ They appear strange.\n把人腿比作烤火腿=觉得样子怪\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 项 They appear vigorous.（显得有活力）是正面评价，但录音用 But 转折后把缠腿比作\n火腿，态度是负面/觉得怪，方向相反。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音前半提到胶带 supposed to help make them thinner（据说能变瘦），C 项 They\nlook charming.（显得迷人）顺着\"变瘦=好看\"的常识把人引向正面评价，但说话人本人的\n评价是火腿比喻的\"怪\"，charming 是被常识带偏的误判。\n借⽤原⽂：supposed to help make them thinner\nD) 过度推断\nD 项 They look unhealthy.（显得不健康）看似贴合\"减肥\"话题，但录音的火腿比喻表达的\n是\"样子怪\"，并没说\"看起来不健康\"；把 strange 推到 unhealthy 是多走了一步的过度推\n断。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker think affects people's interpretation of beauty?", "options": ["A) Culture and upbringing.", "B) Wealth and social status.", "C) Peer pressure.", "D) Media influence."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Culture and upbringing.\n【定位】It is most certainly shaped by a person's culture as well as how they were raised in\ntheir childhood.\n这（对美的看法）无疑是由一个人的文化以及他们童年时的成长方式所塑造的。\n【信号】因果信号 shaped by 后接成因。It is most certainly shaped by a person's culture as\nwell as how they were raised in their childhood 里的 shaped by 是明确的归因信号，\nmost certainly 又加重了语气，shaped by 后面紧跟的 culture 与 how they were raised\n就是答案。\n【替换】a person's culture ↔ Culture and upbringing.\na person's culture = Culture\nhow they were raised in their childhood ↔ Culture and upbringing.\nhow they were raised in childhood = upbringing\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 项 Wealth and social status. 听上去像合理成因，但录音的归因句只说 culture 和\nchildhood，从头到尾没提财富或社会地位，属无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 5 段确有 this pressure to look good 和\"男性也受影响\"，C 项 Peer pressure. 借\n这个 pressure 引诱选，但那讲的是\"想变好看的压力\"，而非\"什么塑造了审美标准\"，且原\n文是 pressure 不是 peer pressure，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：this pressure to look good\nD) 过度推断\n录音开头提到产品由 very slim models 代言，让人联想到媒体影响，D 项 Media\ninfluence. 正利用这种联想，但归因句明确说是 culture 与 upbringing，从未把审美归于\n媒体，属过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：very slim models", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker mainly talk about?", "options": ["A) The relation between hair and skin.", "B) The growing interest in skin studies.", "C) The color of human skin.", "D) The need of skin protection."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） The color of human skin.\n【定位】As a zoologist focusing on the studies of apes and monkeys, I've been studying why humans\nevolved to become the naked ape, and why skin comes in so many different shades around\nthe world.\n作为一名专注研究猿和猴的动物学家，我一直在研究人类为何进化成了裸猿，以及为何全世\n界人类的肤色会有如此多不同的深浅。\n【信号】开头自述研究方向即点题。讲者用 I've been studying why humans evolved to become\nthe naked ape, and why skin comes in so many different shades 自报研究主题，开头\n的研究自述句就是全篇主旨落点，后文各段都在回答这两个 why。\n【替换】why skin comes in so many different shades around the world ↔ The color of human skin.\ndifferent shades=color of skin\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n文中确实多次把 hair 与 skin 对照，听到就想选 The relation between hair and skin.，但\n毛发只是解释肤色变深的铺垫，不是全篇主题，属以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：we mostly lost their coat of fur\nB) 未提及\nThe growing interest in skin studies. 说皮肤研究兴趣日增，原文只说讲者自己在研究皮\n肤，从未提整个领域兴趣上升，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\n文中提过深色皮肤可当天然防晒（a natural sun protector），听到 protector 易选 The\nneed of skin protection.，但防护只是肤色变深的一个功能，不是全篇主旨，属以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：to serve as a natural sun protector", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What had probably caused humans to lose most of their hair one and a half million years ago?", "options": ["A) The necessity to save energy.", "B) Adaptation to the hot environment.", "C) The need to breathe with ease.", "D) Dramatic climate changes on earth."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Adaptation to the hot environment.\n【定位】We think we lost this hair because of the need to keep ourselves cool when we were moving\naround vigorously in a hot environment.\n我们认为之所以失去毛发，是因为在炎热环境中剧烈活动时需要让身体保持凉爽。\n【信号】因果词because引出原因。because of the need to keep ourselves cool when we were\nmoving around vigorously in a hot environment 里的 because of 是明确的因果信号，\n它后面这句就是答案句。\n【替换】the need to keep ourselves cool when we were moving around vigorously in a hot environment ↔\nAdaptation to the hot environment.\n保持凉爽=适应炎热环境\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThe necessity to save energy. 说为节省能量，原文讲的是为散热降温（keep cool），从\n未提省能量，属未提及。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文提到 We can't really lose heat by breathing quickly，听到 breathe 易选 The need\nto breathe with ease.，但原文是说人不能靠喘气散热，故才出汗失毛发，与轻松呼吸正相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：We can't really lose heat by breathing quickly and loudly like dogs\nD) 未提及\nDramatic climate changes on earth. 说地球气候剧变，原文只说在炎热环境中活动需降\n温，没说气候发生剧烈变化，属未提及兼过度推断。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the speaker say protected early humans from the sun?", "options": ["A) Leaves and grass.", "B) Man-made shelter.", "C) Their skin coloring.", "D) Hair on their skin."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Their skin coloring.\n【定位】Basically, we turned our skin darker to serve as a natural sun protector in the place of the hair\nwe lost.\n基本上，我们让皮肤变深，以作为天然的防晒物，取代我们失去的毛发。\n【信号】serve as点明承担保护功能。we turned our skin darker to serve as a natural sun\nprotector in the place of the hair we lost 里 serve as a natural sun protector 直接点明\n承担防晒功能的是变深的皮肤，in the place of the hair 又排除了毛发。\n【替换】we turned our skin darker to serve as a natural sun protector ↔ Their skin coloring.\n变深的皮肤=肤色\n【排除】A) 未提及 Leaves and grass. 树叶草丛，原文从未提早期人类用植物遮阳，属未提及。\nB) 未提及\nMan-made shelter. 人造遮蔽物，原文讲的是早期人类靠身体本身（皮肤）防晒，没提人造\n设施，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文反复说 hair，且 Most animals protect themselves from the sun with fur 易让人选\nHair on their skin.，但原文紧接着说人类不同、是靠皮肤着色，且 in the place of the\nhair we lost 已说明毛发是被取代的，不是答案。\n借⽤原⽂：in the place of the hair we lost", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What happened after humans migrated from Africa to other parts of the world?", "options": ["A) Their genetic makeup began to change.", "B) Their communities began to grow steadily.", "C) Their children began to mix with each other.", "D) Their pace of evolution began to quicken."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Their genetic makeup began to change.\n【定位】These early ancestors of modern Eurasians dispersed into parts of the world that had more\nseasonal sunshine and much lower levels of sun radiation. It's in these populations that we\nbegin to see real changes in the genetic makeup of natural coloring.\n这些现代欧亚人的早期祖先扩散到了阳光更具季节性、太阳辐射水平低得多的地区。正是在\n这些人群中，我们开始看到天然肤色的基因构成发生了真正的变化。\n【信号】扩散之后begin to see变化。原文先讲祖先 dispersed into 别处，紧接着用 we begin to\nsee real changes in the genetic makeup of natural coloring 给出迁徙之后的结果，\nbegin to see 后那句即答案。\n【替换】we begin to see real changes in the genetic makeup of natural coloring ↔\nTheir genetic makeup began to change.\nchanges in genetic makeup=genetic makeup began to change\n【排除】B) 未提及\nTheir communities began to grow steadily. 说社群稳步壮大，原文讲的是迁徙后基因变\n化，从未提社群规模增长，属未提及。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文末段确有 having children with new mixtures of skin color genes，听到\nmix/children 易选 Their children began to mix with each other.，但那是讲今天大城市\n的混居，不是迁出非洲之后那个时点，时间张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：new mixtures of people coming together and having children\nD) 过度推断\nTheir pace of evolution began to quicken. 说进化加速，原文只说看到基因构成变化，从\n未说进化速度变快，属过度推断。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) minimum\n名词\n【词性槽】be kept to a ___ 结构里 a 后缺名词\n【依据】固定搭配 keep sth to a minimum\npasta be kept to a __(26)__, to cut calories, prevent fat build-up and stop blood\nsugar __(27)__ up——目的是减少卡路里、防脂肪堆积、稳血糖，正是『把意面摄入控制在\n最低』的语义，keep ... to a minimum 是地道固定搭配。\n【竞争词】K) ration ration 偏战时/物资配给语境\nration 指被分配的『定量配给』，多用于物资紧缺时的配给制；keep to a ration 不是常见搭\n配，与营养师建议减量的语境不贴。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be kept to a ___ 结构里"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) shooting\n动词\n【词性槽】stop sth ___ up，stop 后宾语 + 动名词或现在分词作宾补\n【依据】shoot up 表『（数值）急剧上升』，正合描述血糖飙升\nto cut calories, prevent fat build-up and stop blood sugar __(27)__ up——并列三个目\n的：减卡、防脂、阻止血糖飙升，stop blood sugar shooting up（阻止血糖飙升）语义完\n全契合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·stop sth ___ up，stop"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) championed\n动词\n【词性槽】has been ___ by clean-eating experts，be done by 被动语态需要过去分词\n【依据】champion 作动词意为『力挺、拥护』，正合 experts 倡导的语境\nMore recently the trend of swapping spaghetti for vegetables has been championed\nby clean-eating experts——『用蔬菜替代意面的潮流被清洁饮食专家所推崇』，与 P2 上\n文 The low-carbohydrate food movement gave birth to such diets as the Atkins,\nPaleo and Keto 关于低碳饮食运动的叙述一脉相承。\n【竞争词】G) lumped 搭配方向不对，且应留给 30 题\nlump 多用在 lump in with（混为一谈），不直接 be lumped by sb；且 30 题 it had been\n__(30)__ in with other ... carbohydrates 空后紧跟 in with，是 lump 的『正穴』。\nH) magnified 语义偏差\nmagnify 是『放大、夸大』，说『潮流被专家放大』勉强能搭、但与 P2『专家积极倡导』的正\n向语境对不齐——上下文是专家在推这套做法，不是在『夸大』它。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— magnified 看似也能填，实则方向反。has been __(28)__\nby clean-eating experts 中 H magnified 词性、被动语态都对，且『被专家放大』勉强能\n解；但 P2 上下文 The low-carbohydrate food movement gave birth to such diets as\nthe Atkins, Paleo and Keto 显示专家在积极倡导这股潮流，应该是 championed（推\n崇），不是 magnified（放大）。防坑：看段落的感情走向，专家在 P2 里是『支持』而非\n『夸大』。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·has been ___ by clea"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) systematic\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 review 之间，缺一个形容词作定语\n【依据】学术固定搭配 systematic review（系统综述）\nP3 说这是 a __(29)__ review and analysis of 30 studies by Canadian researchers——对\n30 项研究做综述与分析，正是学术领域里 systematic review（系统综述）的标准表达，与\n下文 found that not only does pasta not cause weight gain 提到的研究结论也契合。\n【竞争词】A) adverse 感情色彩与全文正面定调相反\nadverse 是负面词（不利的），而本段是用这项研究为意面平反，整体是正面定调；而且\nadverse review（差评）与下文 not only does pasta not cause weight gain 的肯定性结论\n矛盾。\nC) clinical 留给 35 题的 clinical trials\nclinical 虽可作形容词，但 P5 Those involved in the __(35)__ trials 那处的 clinical trials（临\n床试验）是更典型的固定搭配，把它留给 35 题更合理；29 题这里若填 clinical review 并非通\n用学术术语。\nD) contrary 留给 34 题的 contrary to 搭配\ncontrary 更常用在 contrary to 结构里，34 题 a small weight loss __(34)__ to concerns 后面\n紧跟 to，是 contrary 的『正穴』，这里不应抢用。\nF) intimate 语义不搭：intimate review 不成立\nintimate 表『亲密的、私人的』，用在『对 30 项研究的综述』上语义不通，不符合学术语境。\nI) minimum 更适合作名词搭配 keep ... to a minimum\nminimum 在本卷里被 26 题 pasta be kept to a __(26)__ 锁定为名词搭配 keep to a\nminimum，不参与本空的形容词竞争。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·冠词 a 与名词 review 之间，缺"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) lumped\n动词\n【词性槽】had been ___ in with，被动语态需过去分词\n【依据】固定搭配 lump in with（混为一谈）\npasta had been unfairly demonized(妖魔化) because it had been lumped in with\nother, more fat-promoting carbohydrates——意面被妖魔化，是因为它被『和其他更易\n致脂肪堆积的碳水化合物混为一谈』，lump in with 是高度固定的短语搭配。\n【竞争词】H) magnified 搭配不成立\nmagnified in with 不是英语固定搭配，magnify 不与 in with 同现；语义上『把意面和其他碳\n水化合物放大在一起』也不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·had been ___ in with"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) contribute\n动词\n【词性槽】didn't ___ to，助动词 didn't 后接动词原形\n【依据】contribute to 表『导致、促成』，是体重增加这种结果型语境的固定搭配\nThe study found that pasta didn't contribute to weight gain or increase in body fat\n——研究发现意面没有『促成』体重增加或体脂上升；contribute to + 结果 是六级阅读和\n写作高频搭配。\n【竞争词】M) subscribe subscribe to 是『赞同观点』，主语不能是食物\nsubscribe to 通常表示『订阅；赞同某一观点/理论』，主语必须是有意识主体（人/机构）；这\n里主语是 pasta（意面），意面无法『赞同体重增加』，逻辑荒谬。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— subscribe to 形似 contribute to，但主语不对。pasta\ndidn't __(31)__ to weight gain 中 M subscribe 是动词原形且能接 to，词性、搭配都过\n关；但 subscribe to 表『订阅/赞同观点』，主语得是人或机构。pasta 是食物，不可能\n『赞同体重增加』。防坑：看到 ___ to 时，先看主语是『有意识主体』还是『客观事物』\n——客观因果优先 contribute to。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·didn't ___ to，助动词 di"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) weighing\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 In 后需要动名词，构成 In ___ the evidence 的状语结构\n【依据】固定搭配 weigh the evidence（权衡证据）\nIn weighing the evidence, we can now say with some confidence that pasta does\nnot have an adverse effect on body weight outcomes——『在权衡了证据之后，我们\n能够有把握地说……』，weigh the evidence 是学术与法律语境的固定搭配，与下文研究者\nconfident 表态衔接自然。\n【竞争词】J) radiating 搭配与语义都不对\nradiate the evidence『辐射证据』在英语里不成立，radiate 多用于 radiate\nheat/light/confidence 之类的搭配，宾语必为可辐射的事物或抽象品质。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 In 后需要动名词，构成 In _"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adverse\n形容词\n【词性槽】an ___ effect 结构，an 与名词 effect 之间缺形容词\n【依据】固定搭配 adverse effect（不利影响）\nwe can now say with some confidence that pasta does not have an adverse effect\non body weight outcomes when it is consumed as part of a healthy dietary pattern\n——意面在健康饮食模式下对体重结果『没有不利影响』，adverse effect 是医学/营养学\n里极常见的固定搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·an ___ effect 结构，an"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) contrary\n形容词\n【词性槽】weight loss ___ to concerns 中需要个能接 to 的形容词\n【依据】固定搭配 contrary to（与……相反）\nIn __(32)__ the evidence, we can now say with some confidence that pasta does not\nhave an __(33)__ effect on body weight outcomes ... In fact, analysis actually showed\na small weight loss contrary to concerns.——人们本来担心吃意面会胖，结果分析显示\n却出现轻微减重，与人们的担忧『恰恰相反』，contrary to concerns 完美贴合。\n【竞争词】F) intimate 搭配与语义都不成立\nintimate to 不是固定搭配，且 intimate 表『亲密』，与 concerns 语义不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·weight loss ___ to c"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adverse", "B) championed", "C) clinical", "D) contrary", "E) contribute", "F) intimate", "G) lumped", "H) magnified", "I) minimum", "J) radiating", "K) ration", "L) shooting", "M) subscribe", "N) systematic", "O) weighing"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) clinical\n形容词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 与名词 trials 之间，缺形容词作定语\n【依据】固定搭配 clinical trials（临床试验）\nThose involved in the __(35)__ trials on average ate 3.3 servings of pasta a week 描述\n受试者饮食安排，正是医学/营养学研究的典型场景；clinical trial 是不可拆的固定术语。\n【竞争词】A) adverse 感情色彩冲突\nadverse trials『不利的试验』语义生硬，且与下文 They lost around half a kilogram 这一肯定\n性结果格调不合；adverse 应留给 33 题的 adverse effect。\nD) contrary 搭配不当且应留给 34 题\ncontrary trials 不是固定搭配，且 contrary 的『正穴』是 34 题 contrary to concerns。\nF) intimate 语义不搭\nintimate trials（亲密的试验）在科研语境里说不通。\nI) minimum 应留给 26 题的名词搭配\nminimum 在 26 题作名词 keep to a minimum，不与本空竞争。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·定冠词 the 与名词 trials 之"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Although online retailing has existed for some twenty years, nearly half of the internet retailers still fail to receive satisfactory feedback from consumers, according to a recent survey.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】二十年 + 近一半网店反馈不佳\n【定位】A recent study graded more than 600 internet retailers on how easy it was for consumers to\nshop, buy, and pay. Almost half of the sites didn't get a passing grade and only 18 percent got\nan A or B.\n一项最近的研究以\"消费者购物、下单、支付的难易程度\"为标准给 600 多家网店打了分。几\n乎一半的网站没有及格，只有 18% 拿到 A 或 B。\n【改写】online retailing has existed for some twenty years ↔\nalmost two decades after Amazon filed the one-click patent\n时间表述的同义改写\nnearly half of the internet retailers still fail to receive satisfactory feedback from consumers ↔\nAlmost half of the sites didn't get a passing grade\n近一半 + 不及格 = 反馈不佳\naccording to a recent survey ↔ A recent study graded more than 600 internet retailers\nsurvey / study 同义替换\n【辨邻】 C C 谈的是 brick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales——实\n体店占总零售的份额；并未涉及网店是否让消费者满意，与题干 fail to receive\nsatisfactory feedback from consumers 没有交集，仅因\"也带百分比\"易诱人。\nMore than 20 years after the internet was opened to commerce, the Census Bureau tells us that\nbrick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales\nA A 关注的是 online retail sales surged 15.2 percent——增长速度；与题干 fail to\nreceive satisfactory feedback from consumers 的\"满意度\"完全是两件事，话题相邻\n但所述事件不同。\nonline retail sales surged 15.2 percent between the first quarter of 2015 and the first quarter of 2016", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Innovative retailers integrate internet technologies with conventional retailing to create new retail models.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】用互联网技术造新零售模式\n【定位】Creative retailers are using the new technologies to innovate just about everything stores do\nfrom managing inventory, to marketing, to getting paid.\n富有创造力的零售商正在用新技术革新店铺所做的几乎一切——从库存管理到营销再到\n收款。\n【改写】Innovative retailers ↔ Creative retailers\ninnovative ↔ creative 同义\nintegrate internet technologies with conventional retailing ↔\nusing the new technologies to innovate just about everything stores do\n把整合写成\"用 X 改造 Y\"\ncreate new retail models ↔ devising retail models that work for people\ncreate ↔ devising 同义\n【辨邻】 H H 讲的是 Apple's massively successful brick-and-mortar-and-glass retail stores\n与 Bonobos 这类创新零售商对老牌零售商构成威胁，落脚在\"对手\"与\"威胁\"；而题干\nQ37 落脚在\"用互联网技术造新模式\"这一抽象方法论判断，更贴 G。\nApple's massively successful brick-and-mortar-and-glass retail stores and Amazon's small steps in\nthe same direction are what should keep old-fashioned retailers awake at night\nL L 强调 the main thing many large retailers have done in response to all this is to\nopen online stores，且 they struggle to make enough money online——这\n是\"被动应对\"，不是 Q37 所说的\"创造新模式\"，话题相邻但情绪和评价相反。\nthe main thing many large retailers have done in response to all this is to open online stores", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Despite what the Census data suggest, the value of physical retail's stocks has been dropping.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】普查数据 vs 股价下跌\n【定位】Of course, the closed stores, depressed executives, and sinking stocks suggest otherwise.\n当然，关店、沮丧的高管以及下跌的股价表明的恰恰相反。\n【改写】Despite what the Census data suggest ↔ the Census data suggest that physical retail is thriving\nCensus data 原样复现\nthe value of physical retail's stocks has been dropping ↔ sinking stocks\ndropping ↔ sinking 同义\n【辨邻】 C C 给的是 brick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales 与\nonly 0.8 percent of retail sales shifted from offline to online——只是 Census 数\n据，没有任何关于股价、关店、高管沮丧的描述，因此不能完整承载题干。\nbrick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales in the first quarter of 2016\nN N 的核心论断是 the Census retail data are unreliable 与 Census simply\nmisclassifies a large chunk of online sales——它是在质疑普查数据的可信度，不是\n在用 Census 数据与股价做对比，与题干 Despite what the Census data suggest 的\n反差结构不一致。\nthe Census retail data are unreliable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Innovative-driven changes in the retail industry didn't take place as quickly as widely anticipated.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】变化没像预期那样快\n【定位】But many widely anticipated changes weren't quick, and some haven't really started.\n但很多被广泛预期的变化并没有那么快发生，有一些甚至还没真正开始。\n【改写】Innovative-driven changes in the retail industry ↔ many widely anticipated changes\n话题等价\ndidn't take place as quickly as widely anticipated ↔\nmany widely anticipated changes weren't quick\n否定式比较句的同义改写\n【辨邻】 F F 强调 conventional retailers' confidence in the future increased，而非变化是否\n快；尽管 internet was going to kill—and quickly 提到\"快\"，但它讲的是\"预言\"本\n身，不是 Q39 所说\"变化没那么快\"。\nconventional retailers' confidence in the future increased as Census continued to report weak\nonline sales\nJ J 关注 The sudden decline in foot traffic in recent years 与 stores are downsizing\nand closing——这是结果性变化，而非把变化速度与预期对比。\nThe sudden decline in foot traffic in recent years", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Statistics indicate that brick and mortar sales still made up the lion's share of the retail business.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】实体仍占绝大多数（lion's share）\n【定位】More than 20 years after the internet was opened to commerce, the Census Bureau tells\nus that brick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales in the first quarter\nof 2016.\n互联网开放商用 20 多年后，人口普查局告诉我们：2016 年第一季度，实体店销售占零售\n总额的 92.3%。\n【改写】Statistics indicate that ↔ the Census Bureau tells us\n数据来源同义\nbrick and mortar sales still made up the lion's share ↔\nbrick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales\nlion's share = 92.3% 的习语对数字\n【辨邻】 A online retail sales surged 15.2 percent 的 15.2% 是网售的增长，与题干 brick and\nmortar sales still made up the lion's share 中\"实体仍占大头\"主体相反，不可对应。\nonline retail sales surged 15.2 percent between the first quarter of 2015 and the first quarter of 2016\nB on a small base of 6.9 percent 是网售自身的小基数，并未直接给出实体占比；它是\n用来削弱 A 涨幅的论据，与题干 Q40 想要的\"实体占大头\"不是同一事实。\nIt was an increase that was on a small base of 6.9 percent", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Companies that successfully combine online and offline business models may prove to be a big concern for traditional retailers.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】成功融合线上线下者成传统零售的隐忧\n【定位】Not to mention the large number of creative new retailers, like Bonobos, that are blending\nonline and offline experiences in creative ways.\n更不用说像 Bonobos 这样大量富有创意的新零售商，正以创造性的方式融合线上线下购物\n体验。\n【改写】Companies that successfully combine online and offline business models ↔\nApple's massively successful brick-and-mortar-and-glass retail stores\n成功融合两端的公司\na big concern for traditional retailers ↔\nwhat should keep old-fashioned retailers awake at night\n夜不能寐 ↔ 担忧\n【辨邻】 G G 只表达 Creative retailers are using the new technologies to innovate just\nabout everything stores do——\"创新方式\"，没有\"威胁传统零售商\"的语义；与题干\na big concern for traditional retailers 不对应。\nCreative retailers are using the new technologies to innovate just about everything stores do from\nmanaging inventory, to marketing, to getting paid\nL L 的主语是 large retailers——他们是被冲击的传统零售商；与 Q41 的主语\"成功融合\n线上线下者（威胁源）\"主体不一致，仅因都谈\"线上 + 传统零售\"而易诱人。\nthe main thing many large retailers have done in response to all this is to open online stores", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Brick and mortar retailers' faith in their business was strengthened when the dot com bubble burst.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】互联网泡沫破灭 + 实体信心增强\n【定位】The dot-com bust discredited most predictions of that sort and in the years that followed,\nconventional retailers' confidence in the future increased as Census continued to report weak\nonline sales.\n互联网泡沫破灭打脸了那一类预言；之后数年里，随着普查局不断报告网售疲软，传统零售\n商对未来的信心愈发增强。\n【改写】Brick and mortar retailers ↔ conventional retailers\nBrick and mortar ↔ conventional\nfaith in their business was strengthened ↔\nconventional retailers' confidence in the future increased\nfaith ↔ confidence + strengthened ↔ increased\nthe dot com bubble burst ↔ The dot-com bust\nbubble burst 的名词化\n【辨邻】 A A 是\"实体衰退\"的当下图景（Retail profits are falling sharply），既不在泡沫破灭后\n这个时段、也不是\"信心增强\"，与题干所述事件无关。\nRetail profits are falling sharply. Stores are closing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Despite the tremendous challenges from online retailing, traditional retailing will be here to stay for quite some time.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】传统零售短期内不会消失\n【定位】Despite the turmoil, brick and mortar won't disappear any time soon.\n尽管乱局重重，实体店在可预见的时间内不会消失。\n【改写】Despite the tremendous challenges from online retailing ↔ Despite the turmoil\n让步连词 + 概括上位\ntraditional retailing will be here to stay for quite some time ↔\nbrick and mortar won't disappear any time soon\n肯定式 = 否定式的同义改写\n【辨邻】 C brick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales in the first\nquarter of 2016 只是当下的份额事实，没有任何\"未来会怎样\"的判断，与 Q43 的未\n来时刻判断不对应。\nbrick and mortar sales accounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales in the first quarter of 2016\nD the Census data suggest that physical retail is thriving 是\"当下繁荣\"，不是\"未来\n仍在\"；与题干 will be here to stay for quite some time 时态不一致。\nthe Census data suggest that physical retail is thriving", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "With the rise of online commerce, physical retail stores are likely to suffer the same fate as the yellow pages.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】黄页式衰亡的比喻\n【定位】Brick and mortar stores(实体店) seem to be going the way of the yellow pages.\n实体店看起来正走上黄页的老路（即被时代淘汰）。\n【改写】With the rise of online commerce ↔\nThe internet is apparently taking down yet another industry\n线上崛起的背景\nphysical retail stores are likely to suffer the same fate as the yellow pages ↔\nBrick and mortar stores(实体店) seem to be going the way of the yellow pages\n同一习语的同义改写\n【辨邻】 O brick and mortar won't disappear any time soon 与题干 suffer the same fate as\nthe yellow pages 是相反判断（一个说不会消失、一个说会被淘汰）；不可对应。\nbrick and mortar won't disappear any time soon", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "The wide use of smartphones has made it more complex for traditional retailers to reinvent their business.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】智能手机普及让传统零售再造更复杂\n【定位】The rise of the mobile phone has recently added a new level of complexity to the process of\nretail reinvention.\n智能手机的兴起最近又给零售业的再造过程增加了一层新的复杂性。\n【改写】The wide use of smartphones ↔ The rise of the mobile phone\nsmartphones ↔ mobile phone + wide use ↔ rise\nmade it more complex for traditional retailers to reinvent their business ↔\nadded a new level of complexity to the process of retail reinvention\n动词式 ↔ 名词化\n【辨邻】 G G 谈的是 a growing array of internet-connected tools——所有联网工具的总和，\n并没有像 K 那样专门指向 mobile phone 这一具体设备及其带来的\"复杂度增加\"。\na growing array of internet-connected tools to change how they search, shop, and buy\nJ People can shop more efficiently online 谈的是\"线上更高效 → 人流降\"，没有\nsmartphones 这一具体工具，也没有 more complex 这一评价——两点都不对应。\nPeople can shop more efficiently online and therefore don't need to go to as many stores\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n用一连串\"哀景\"开篇——零售利润大跌、店铺关门、商场空置，把实体店比作正在被\n互联网\"做掉\"的行业，并抛出人口普查局的数据：2015 一季度到 2016 一季度网络零\n售涨了 15.2 percent。\n信息指纹 黄页式衰亡的比喻 + 15.2% 网售涨幅\nB\n提醒读者别被 15.2% 这个\"猛涨\"误导：它是建立在 a small base of 6.9 percent 之上的\n小基数高百分比，再大的涨幅落在很小的盘子上仍然很小。\n信息指纹 小基数 6.9% 的辩证提醒\nC\n继续摆数据替实体店\"翻案\"：互联网开放商用 20 多年后，brick and mortar sales\naccounted for 92.3 percent of retail sales；过去一年只有 0.8 percent of retail sales\nshifted from offline to online。\n信息指纹 92.3% 实体占比 / 0.8% 迁移量\nD 把表面景象与数据放在一起对比：尽管有无人机送货的喧嚣、高管焦虑、总统候选人\n喊话亚马逊有 huge antitrust problem，the Census data suggest that physical retail is\nthriving；但关店、高管沮丧、股价下跌又指向相反结论——\"真实故事到底是什么\"。\n信息指纹 普查数据看似繁荣 vs 股价下跌的反差\nE\n承认许多实体零售商确实陷入困境，把行业被重塑比作熊彼特意义上的 a gale of\ncreative destruction；连梅西百货的 CFO 都说 We're frankly scratching our heads。\n信息指纹 创造性破坏之风 + 梅西 CFO 挠头\nF\n回顾互联网泡沫年代：曾预言网络很快杀死实体店，结果泡沫破灭后这类预言被打\n脸；接下来几年人口普查不断报告网售疲软，conventional retailers' confidence in the\nfuture increased，直到\"大风\"真的来。\n信息指纹 dot-com 泡沫破灭后传统零售商信心增强\nG\n提出重新发明零售业不是\"砖头 vs 点击\"的你死我活，而是要为越来越多使用互联网工\n具的消费者设计新模式：Creative retailers are using the new technologies to\ninnovate just about everything stores do——从库存管理、营销到收款。\n信息指纹 用新技术革新零售的所有环节\nH 真正应让传统零售商\"夜不能寐\"的不是无人机送内裤，而是苹果成功的实体店、亚马\n逊向实体迈出的小步，以及 Bonobos 这类在 blending online and offline experiences\nin creative ways 的新型零售商。\n信息指纹 线上线下融合的创新零售商让老牌零售商夜不能寐\nI 强调零售业的再造\"不是按互联网时间发生\"：有些变化的确快（如 Craigslist quickly\novertook newspaper classified ads），但很多广为预期的变化并没有那么快，互联网\n将以电力等大发明的节奏改造经济。\n信息指纹 零售再造没有按预期那样快发生 + Craigslist/电力类比\nJ\n大风仍在刮：近几年人流量骤降是关键警报；人们因为线上更高效，不必逛那么多\n店，There's a surplus of physical shopping space for the crowds，所以店在缩、\n在关。\n信息指纹 人流骤降 + 实体商业面积过剩\nK 智能手机的普及给零售再造又加了一层复杂度：五年前消费者要么在电脑前买、\n要么去商场看；如今手机几乎随时联网，Even when a retailer gets a customer to\nwalk in the store, she can easily see if there's a better deal online or at another\nstore nearby。\n信息指纹 智能手机给传统零售再造再加一层复杂度\nL 迄今为止大型零售商对此的主要回应只是\"开网店\"，结果遇到和报纸一样的困境：即\n使有线上流量，they struggle to make enough money online to compensate for what\nthey are losing offline。\n信息指纹 开网店补不上线下流失\nM 举沃尔玛为例做得相对好但其线上同比也只涨 7%，CEO 哀叹 Growth here is too\nslow；并引述一项针对 600 多家网店的研究：Almost half of the sites didn't get a\npassing grade and only 18 percent got an A or B。\n信息指纹 600+ 网店评测：近一半不及格，仅 18% 拿 A 或 B\nN\n实体的乱局难与普查数据自洽：作者深挖发现 the Census retail data are unreliable\n——很可能把大量线上销售误分类，把沃尔玛等传统零售商的线上销售与 non-store\nretailers 如食品车混在一起，掩盖了真实变化。\n信息指纹 普查零售数据不可靠 + 食品车与传统线上销售混算\nO\n尽管动荡，brick and mortar won't disappear any time soon；真正的悬念是十年二十\n年后哪些传统零售商还在场，新玩家是谁，每个品类的购物体验如何变化；投资者别\n贸然把实体店\"勾掉\"。\n信息指纹 实体店短期内不会消失 / 别勾掉实体", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What did Stephen Hawking think of artificial intelligence?", "options": ["A) It would be vital to the progress of human civilisation.", "B) It might be a blessing or a disaster in the making.", "C) It might present challenges as well as opportunities.", "D) It would be a significant expansion of human intelligence."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did Stephen Hawking think of artificial intelligence? 问的是某人对某事物的\n具体看法，要求回原文找观点句。\n【拆句】题干 What did Stephen Hawking think of artificial intelligence? 是 wh-疑问句，主干\n「X think of Y」，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Professor Stephen Hawking has warned that the creation of powerful artificial intelligence\n(AI) will be \"either the best, or the worst thing, ever to happen to humanity\".\n霍金教授警告说，创造强大的人工智能将会是「发生在人类身上最好或最坏的事」。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nvital to the progress of human civilisation 只取了「好」的一面，丢掉了 worst\nthing 的另一面。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe best, or the worst thing ↔ a blessing or a disaster\never to happen to humanity ↔ in the making\n预判「可能最好可能最坏」，与 a blessing or a disaster in the making 在结构和语义\n上都完全对应，锁定 B。\nC) 范围程度偷改\nchallenges as well as opportunities 听起来很对，但 challenges 远弱于 worst\nthing，opportunities 远弱于 best thing，程度被显著削弱。\nD) 答非所问\na significant expansion of human intelligence 与 our own minds are amplified by\nAI 近似，但那是 Q46 之外的内容，且 Q46 问的是首段的总体评价，不是 P3 的局部\n细节。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did Hawking say about the creation of the LCFI?", "options": ["A) It would accelerate the progress of AI research.", "B) It would mark a step forward in the AI industry.", "C) It was extremely important to the destiny of humankind.", "D) It was an achievement of multi-disciplinary collaboration."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did Hawking say about the creation of the LCFI? 问具体陈述，走常规细节\n定位。\n【拆句】What did Hawking say about the creation of the LCFI?，主干 「X say about Y」，\ncreation of the LCFI 作介词宾语，结构简单。\n【定位】praised the creation of an academic institute dedicated to researching the future of\nintelligence as \"crucial to the future of our civilisation and our species\".\n（霍金）赞扬创立一所致力于研究未来智能的学术机构，称其「对我们文明和物种的未来至\n关重要」。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nHawking 只说该机构对人类未来至关重要，并未提及它会加速 AI 研究的进度。\nB) 偷换概念\nHawking 强调对 civilisation 与 species 重要，不是对 AI industry 重要。\nC) ✓ 正确\ncrucial ↔ extremely important\nthe future of our civilisation and our species ↔ the destiny of humankind\n预判「对人类未来至关重要」，与 extremely important to the destiny of\nhumankind 在程度和对象上完全对应，锁定 C。\nD) 答非所问\n原文 multi-disciplinary institute 只是描述机构属性，并非 Hawking 对 LCFI 的评价；\nHawking 评价的是其重要性，不是其合作成就。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What did Hawking say was a welcome change in AI research?", "options": ["A) The shift of research focus from the past to the future.", "B) The shift of research from theory to implementation.", "C) The greater emphasis on the negative impact of AI.", "D) The increasing awareness of mankind's past stupidity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did Hawking say was a welcome change in AI research? 是问 Hawking 所\n说的具体内容，定位句一句话即可作答。\n【拆句】What did Hawking say was a welcome change in AI research? 含套层从句：主句\nWhat did Hawking say，宾语从句省略主语 「(what) was a welcome change」。即问\nHawking 所说的「那个 welcome change」是什么。\n【定位】\"We spend a great deal of time studying in history,\" Hawking said, \"which, let's face it, is\nmostly the history of stupidity. So it's a welcome change that people are studying instead the\nfuture of intelligence.\"\n「我们花大量时间研究历史，」霍金说，「而历史多半是一部愚蠢史。所以人们改去研究未\n来智能，是一个可喜的变化。」\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nstudying in history ↔ from the past\nstudying instead the future of intelligence ↔ to the future\nwelcome change ↔ shift of research focus\n预判「研究对象由历史转向未来」，与 The shift of research focus from the past to\nthe future. 完全对位，锁定 A。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈的是「历史 vs 未来」，与「理论 vs 实施」完全不在一个维度。\nC) 偷换概念\nHawking 称可喜变化是研究 future of intelligence，而不是着重 AI 的负面影响。\nD) 答非所问\n原文 mostly the history of stupidity 只是 Hawking 的一句吐槽，用来对比为何研究未\n来反而是 welcome change，并不是 welcome change 本身。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What concerns did Hawking raise about AI?", "options": ["A) It may exceed human intelligence sooner or later.", "B) It may ultimately over-amplify the human mind.", "C) Super-intelligence may cause its own destruction.", "D) Super-intelligence may eventually ruin mankind."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What concerns did Hawking raise about AI? 是细节，答 Hawking 担忧的具体\n内容。\n【拆句】What concerns did Hawking raise about AI? 主干 「X raise concerns about Y」，\nraise 是搭配动词，about AI 指明话题范围。结构清晰。\n【定位】the world-renowned physicist has often been cautious about AI, raising concerns that\nhumanity could be the architect of its own destruction if it creates a super-intelligence with a\nwill of its own.\n这位享誉世界的物理学家在 AI 问题上常常小心翼翼，他担忧：如果人类造出有自己意志的\n超级智能，人类可能会成为自我毁灭的设计师。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nHawking 谈的是自我意志的超级智能毁灭人类，不是 AI 何时迟早超过人类智能的时间\n预测。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 our own minds are amplified by AI 是正面预期，B 选项加了 over- 变成负面\nover-amplify，与原文相反。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是 super-intelligence 毁灭人类（destruction of humanity），C 改成 super-\nintelligence 毁灭 its own——主体宾语反了。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhumanity could be the architect of its own destruction ↔ ruin mankind\nsuper-intelligence with a will of its own ↔ Super-intelligence\nif it creates ↔ eventually\n预判「超级智能或将毁灭人类」，与 Super-intelligence may eventually ruin\nmankind. 完全对位，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do we learn about some entrepreneurs from the technology industry?", "options": ["A) They are much influenced by the academic community.", "B) They are most likely to benefit from AI development.", "C) They share the same concerns about AI as academics.", "D) They believe they can keep AI under human control."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about some entrepreneurs from the technology industry? 问的是\n关于该群体的具体陈述，走细节路径。\n【拆句】What do we learn about some entrepreneurs from the technology industry? 主干\n「learn about X」；from the technology industry 是后置定语修饰 entrepreneurs。无\n陷阱。\n【定位】The academic community is not alone in warning about the potential dangers of AI as well as\nthe potential benefits. A number of pioneers from the technology industry, most famously the\nentrepreneur Elon Musk, have also expressed their concerns about the damage that a super-\nintelligent AI could do to humanity.\n学术界并非唯一发出 AI 警示的群体。来自科技业的一批先驱（其中最著名的是企业家埃隆·\n马斯克）也表达了对超级智能 AI 可能给人类带来损害的担忧。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只说两群体不约而同地担忧，并未说企业家是受学术界影响才担忧的——立场一致\n≠ 受影响。\nB) 无中生有\n原文未提及科技业企业家「最可能受益于 AI 发展」，纯属臆造。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThe academic community is not alone in warning ↔\nshare the same concerns about AI as academics\nhave also expressed their concerns ↔ share the same concerns\nacademic community ↔ academics\n预判「与学术界同样担忧」，与 They share the same concerns about AI as\nacademics. 在立场、对象、群体三方面都对位，锁定 C。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文表达的是科技业企业家担忧 AI 危害，D 却说他们相信能控制 AI——方向反转。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the passage say about the startups?", "options": ["A) They never lose time in upgrading products for seniors.", "B) They want to have a share of the seniors' goods market.", "C) They invite seniors to their companies to try their products.", "D) They try to profit from promoting digital products to seniors."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about the startups 直问「文章关于 X 说了什么」，指\n向原文某句客观陈述。\n【拆句】题干 What does the passage say about the startups 是简单特殊疑问句，主干 the\npassage say what about the startups，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The market for products designed specifically for older adults could reach $30 billion by next\nyear, and startups want in on the action.\n专门为老年人设计的产品市场明年可能达到 300 亿美元，初创公司想要在这块蛋糕里分一\n杯羹。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项 They never lose time in upgrading products for seniors 说「绝不浪费时间升\n级产品」，原文从未提及「升级速度」或 never lose time 这一信息。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThe market for products designed specifically for older adults could reach \\$30 billion by next\nyear\n↔ the seniors' goods market\nstartups want in on the action ↔ want to have a share of\n预判「初创公司想进入老年市场分蛋糕」，与 They want to have a share of the\nseniors' goods market 在话题（老年市场）和动作（分一份）两个维度上完全等值，\n无其他选项能同时满足。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 They invite seniors to their companies to try their products 说创业公司请老人\n去公司，但原文 Brookdale, the country's largest owner of retirement\ncommunities, has been inviting a few select entrepreneurs just to move in for a\nfew days 是养老社区 Brookdale 邀请创业者入住社区，方向完全相反。\nD) 以偏概全\n选项 They try to profit from promoting digital products to seniors 把范围缩成「数\n字产品」，但原文 products designed specifically for older adults 并未限定为数字产\n品；后文也提到全身吹风机、自助穿衣这类非数字产品。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Some entrepreneurs have been invited to Brookdale to______.", "options": ["A) have an interview with potential customers", "B) conduct a survey of retirement communities", "C) collect residents' feedback on their products", "D) show senior residents how to use IT products"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Some entrepreneurs have been invited to Brookdale to 是不完整陈述句，问目\n的，需要从原文找邀请的目的状语。\n【拆句】题干 Some entrepreneurs have been invited to Brookdale to 是被动语态待补全句，\nhave been invited to do 中 to 后面接选项补完，考生须把选项当作目的状语来读。\n【定位】So Brookdale, the country's largest owner of retirement communities, has been inviting a few\nselect entrepreneurs just to move in for a few days, show off their products and hear what the\nresidents have to say.\n因此，全美最大的养老社区运营商 Brookdale 一直邀请少数精选的创业者入住几天，展示\n产品，听取居民的意见。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项 have an interview with potential customers 把「听取反馈」偷换成「采访潜在\n客户」；原文 Brookdale 居民是「试用者」，且交流方式是聊天、玩牌等非正式途径\n（Playing pool, playing cards, having dinner），不是正式采访。\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 conduct a survey of retirement communities 把调查对象错挂到「养老社区」\n上；原文要求创业者听取的是居民对产品的反馈，调查对象是产品，不是社区。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhear what the residents have to say ↔ collect residents' feedback\nshow off their products ↔ on their products\n预判中「听取居民意见」是邀请的根本目的，collect residents' feedback on their\nproducts 正是这个目的的标准书面表达，其他选项要么动作错（A、B、D），要么对\n象错。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 show senior residents how to use IT products 说创业者来「教老人用 IT 产\n品」；原文方向相反，是创业者来「听老人怎么说自己的产品」，且产品类型不限\n于 IT。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we know about SentabTV?", "options": ["A) It is a TV program catering to the interest of the elderly.", "B) It is a digital TV which enjoys popularity among seniors.", "C) It is a TV specially designed for seniors to view programs.", "D) It is a communication system via TV instead of a computer."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we know about SentabTV 问对 SentabTV 已知什么，找原文对该产品的\n客观描述句。\n【拆句】题干 What do we know about SentabTV 结构简单，主干 we know what about\nSentabTV，无陷阱。\n【定位】The startup's product, SentabTV, enables older adults who may not be comfortable with\ncomputers to access email, video chat and social media using just their televisions and a\nremote control.\n这家初创公司的产品 SentabTV，让那些不熟悉电脑的老年人，只用电视和遥控器就能收发\n邮件、视频聊天和使用社交媒体。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项 It is a TV program catering to the interest of the elderly 把 SentabTV 偷换为\n「电视节目」；原文是产品（通讯系统），不是节目。\nB) 无中生有\n选项 It is a digital TV which enjoys popularity among seniors 说「数字电视且广受\n老人欢迎」；原文既未说它是数字电视，也未提及「广受欢迎」——恰恰相反，P7 中\nBusch 表示她不需要。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 It is a TV specially designed for seniors to view programs 说 SentabTV 是\n「专为老人看节目设计的电视」；原文是「通讯系统」，不是看节目的电视。\nD) ✓ 正确\nenables older adults who may not be comfortable with computers to access email, video chat and\nsocial media\n↔ a communication system\nusing just their televisions and a remote control ↔ via TV instead of a computer\n预判的「功能（通讯）+ 介质（电视）+ 对比项（电脑）」三要素，只有 It is a\ncommunication system via TV instead of a computer 同时覆盖；其他三项要么对象\n错，要么属性错。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does Rodriguez say is important in promoting products?", "options": ["A) Winning trust from prospective customers.", "B) Knowing the likes and dislikes of customers.", "C) Demonstrating their superiority on the spot.", "D) Responding promptly to customer feedback."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does Rodriguez say is important in promoting products 问 Rodriguez 的\n原话，是原文直接引语的转述。\n【拆句】题干 What does Rodriguez say is important in promoting products 中 What 既是 say\n的宾语从句主语，又是 is important 的主语，结构是 What [is important in promoting\nproducts] does Rodriguez say？考生易把它读成两个分立的疑问，实际只问一件事：什么\n东西在推销中重要。\n【定位】When they get to know me and to trust me, knowing for sure I'm not selling them something\n—there'll be more honest feedback from them.\n当他们开始了解我、信任我，确信我不是来卖东西的——就会有更诚实的反馈。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nto trust me ↔ Winning trust\nresidents ↔ prospective customers\n整段两次出现 trust / not selling 的对立，trust 是 Rodriguez 反复强调的核心；\nWinning trust from prospective customers 是这一核心的精准书面表达。\nB) 无中生有\n选项 Knowing the likes and dislikes of customers 说「了解客户喜好」；原文\nRodriguez 强调的是「信任」，不是「了解喜好」——尽管聊汉堡配料、玩牌看似在了\n解喜好，但 Rodriguez 自己点名的核心是 trust。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Demonstrating their superiority on the spot 说「当场展示产品优势」；恰恰相\n反，原文 residents here don't feel like he's selling them something 强调不能让人觉\n得在推销，更不能当场炫耀。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 Responding promptly to customer feedback 说「及时回应反馈」；原文\nRodriguez 谈的是「如何拿到诚实反馈」，没有谈「拿到之后如何回应」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What do we learn about the seniors in the Brookdale community?", "options": ["A) Most of them are interested in using the Sentab.", "B) They are quite at ease with high-tech products.", "C) They have much in common with seniors elsewhere.", "D) Most of them enjoy a longer life than average people."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about the seniors in the Brookdale community 问对该社区老\n人已知什么，找原文对该群体的特征描述句。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about the seniors in the Brookdale community 中 in the\nBrookdale community 是修饰 seniors 的后置介词短语，限定为「这一个社区里的老\n人」，考生须读出限定关系，不要泛化成「所有老人」。\n【定位】But Rodriguez says he's still learning something important by moving into this Brookdale\ncommunity: \"People are more tech-proficient than we thought.\"\n但 Rodriguez 说他入住这个 Brookdale 社区还学到了一件重要的事：「人们比我们想象的\n更精通科技」。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n选项 Most of them are interested in using the Sentab 说「大多数人对 Sentab 感兴\n趣」；原文 She tells Rodriguez that it might be good for someone, but not for\nher 表明个案明确表示自己不需要，全文无任何「多数感兴趣」的证据。\nB) ✓ 正确\nPeople are more tech-proficient than we thought ↔\nThey are quite at ease with high-tech products\nMany residents have backgrounds in engineering, business and academic circles ↔\nquite at ease with high-tech products\n预判「该社区老人比想象中更懂科技」，与 They are quite at ease with high-tech\nproducts 在评价对象（科技产品）和评价程度（quite/more than thought）两个维度\n完全等值，独此一项。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 They have much in common with seniors elsewhere 说「与他处老人有很多共\n同点」；原文 To be fair, if Rodriguez had wanted feedback from some more\ntechnophobic seniors, he might have ended up in the wrong Brookdale\ncommunity 恰恰是说这一社区特殊、不代表别处。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 Most of them enjoy a longer life than average people 说「多数人比常人长\n寿」；全文从未提及寿命，唯一年龄数字 Mary Lou Busch, 93 是个案，不能推出群体\n寿命结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
